\h John
\toc1 John
\toc2 John
\toc3 JHN
\mt1 John
\c 1
\s1 The Beginning
\r (\ref Genesis 1:1–2|GEN 1:1-2\ref*; \ref Hebrews 11:1–3|HEB 11:1-3\ref*)
\p \v 1 In \add the\add* beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. \v 2 He was with God in \add the\add* beginning. \v 3 Through Him all things were made, and without Him nothing was made that has been made. \v 4 In Him was life, and \add that\add* life was the light of men. \v 5 The Light shines in the darkness, and the darkness \add has\add* not overcome\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Or \fqa comprehended\f* it.
\s1 The Witness of John
\p \v 6 There came a man who was sent from God. His name \add was\add* John. \v 7 He came as a witness to testify about the Light, so that through him everyone might believe. \v 8 He himself was not the Light, but he came to testify about the Light.
\p \v 9 The true Light, who gives light to everyone, was coming into the world. \v 10 He was in the world, and \add though\add* the world was made through Him, the world \add did\add* not recognize Him. \v 11 He came to \add His\add* own, and \add His\add* own \add did\add* not receive Him. \v 12 But to all who did receive Him, to those who believed in His name, He gave the right to become children of God — \v 13 \add children born\add* not of blood, nor of \add the\add* desire \add or\add* will of man, but born of God.
\s1 The Word Became Flesh
\r (\ref Psalm 84:1–12|PSA 84:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 14 The Word became flesh and made His dwelling among us.\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Or \fqa and tabernacled among us\f* We have seen His glory, \add the\add* glory of \add the\add* one and only \add Son\add*\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Or \fqa the Only Begotten \ft or \fqa the Unique One\f* from \add the\add* Father, full of grace and truth.
\p \v 15 John testified concerning Him. He cried out, saying, “This is He of whom I said, ‘He who comes after me has surpassed me because He was before me.’”
\p \v 16 From His fullness we \add have\add* all received grace upon grace. \v 17 For the law was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ. \v 18 No one has ever seen God, \add but\add* the one and only Son, \add who is Himself\add* God \add and\add*\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Or \fqa but the only begotten God, who\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa but the only begotten Son, who\f* is at the Father’s side,\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Greek \fqa in the Father’s bosom\f* has made \add Him\add* known.
\s1 The Mission of John the Baptist
\r (\ref Isaiah 40:1–5|ISA 40:1-5\ref*; \ref Matthew 3:1–12|MAT 3:1-12\ref*; \ref Mark 1:1–8|MRK 1:1-8\ref*; \ref Luke 3:1–20|LUK 3:1-20\ref*)
\p \v 19 And this was John’s testimony when the Jews of Jerusalem sent priests and Levites to ask him, “Who are you?” \v 20 He did not refuse to confess, but openly declared, “I am not the Christ.”
\p \v 21 “Then who \add are you\add*?” they inquired. “Are you Elijah?”
\p He said, “I am not.”
\p “Are you the Prophet?”
\p He answered, “No.”
\p \v 22 So they said to him, “Who are you? We need an answer for those who sent us. What do you say about yourself?”
\p \v 23 \add John\add* replied in the words of Isaiah the prophet:
\b 
\q1 “I \add am\add* a voice of one calling in the wilderness,
\q2 ‘Make straight the way for \add the\add* Lord.’”\f + \fr 1:23 \ref Isaiah 40:3|ISA 40:3\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 24 Then the Pharisees who had been sent \v 25 asked him, “Why then do you baptize, if you are not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet?”
\p \v 26 “I baptize with\f + \fr 1:26 \ft Or \fqa in\ft ; also in verse 31 and twice in 33\f* water,” John replied, “but among you stands \add One\add* you \add do\add* not know. \v 27 \add He is\add* the One who comes after me, the straps of whose sandals I am not worthy to untie.”
\p \v 28 All this happened at Bethany beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing.
\s1 Jesus the Lamb of God
\r (\ref Matthew 3:13–17|MAT 3:13-17\ref*; \ref Mark 1:9–11|MRK 1:9-11\ref*; \ref Luke 3:21–22|LUK 3:21-22\ref*)
\p \v 29 The next day \add John\add* saw Jesus coming toward him and said, “Look, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world! \v 30 This is He of whom I said, ‘A man who comes after me has surpassed me because He was before me.’ \v 31 I myself \add did\add* not know Him, but the reason I came baptizing with water \add was\add* that He might be revealed to Israel.”
\p \v 32 Then John testified, “I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove and resting on Him. \v 33 I myself \add did\add* not know Him, but the \add One who\add* sent me to baptize with water told me, ‘\add The man\add* on whom you see the Spirit descend and rest is He who will baptize with \add the\add* Holy Spirit.’ \v 34 I have seen and testified that this is the Son of God.\f + \fr 1:34 \ft SBL \fqa the Chosen One of God\f*”
\s1 The First Disciples
\r (\ref Matthew 4:18–22|MAT 4:18-22\ref*; \ref Mark 1:16–20|MRK 1:16-20\ref*; \ref Luke 5:1–11|LUK 5:1-11\ref*)
\p \v 35 The next day John was there again with two of his disciples. \v 36 When he saw Jesus walking by, he said, “Look, the Lamb of God!” \v 37 And when the two disciples heard him say this, they followed Jesus.
\p \v 38 Jesus turned and saw them following. \wj “What do you want?”\wj* He asked.
\p They said to Him, “Rabbi” (which means Teacher), “where are You staying?”
\p \v 39 \wj “Come and see,”\wj* He replied. So they went and saw where He was staying, and spent that day with Him. It was about \add the\add* tenth hour.\f + \fr 1:39 \ft That is, about four in the afternoon\f*
\p \v 40 Andrew, Simon Peter’s brother, was one of the two who heard John’s  \add testimony \add* and followed \add Jesus\add*. \v 41 He first found \add his\add* brother Simon and told him, “We have found the Messiah” (which is translated as Christ).
\p \v 42 \add Andrew\add* brought him to Jesus, \add who\add* looked at him \add and\add* said, \wj “You are Simon son of John. You will be called Cephas”\wj* (which is translated as Peter).
\s1 Jesus Calls Philip and Nathanael
\p \v 43 The next day \add Jesus\add* decided to set out for Galilee. Finding Philip, \add He\add* told him, \wj “Follow Me.”\wj* \v 44 Now Philip was from Bethsaida, the \add same\add* town as Andrew and Peter.
\p \v 45 Philip found Nathanael and told him, “We have found the \add One\add* Moses wrote about in the Law, \add the One\add* the prophets \add foretold\add* — Jesus of Nazareth, \add the\add* son of Joseph.”
\p \v 46 “Can anything good come from Nazareth?” Nathanael asked.
\p “Come and see,” said Philip.
\p \v 47 \add When\add* Jesus saw Nathanael approaching, He said of him, \wj “Here \add is\add* a true Israelite, in whom there is no deceit.”\wj*
\p \v 48 “How do You know me?” Nathanael asked.
\p Jesus replied, \wj “Before Philip called you, I saw you under the fig tree.”\wj*
\p \v 49 “Rabbi,” Nathanael answered, “You are the Son of God! You are the King of Israel!”
\p \v 50 Jesus said to him, \wj “Do you believe just because I told you I saw you under the fig tree? You will see greater things than these.”\wj* \v 51 Then He declared, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, you will all see heaven open and the angels of God ascending and descending on the Son of Man.”\wj*\f + \fr 1:51 \ft See \ref Genesis 28:12|GEN 28:12\ref*.\f*
\c 2
\s1 The Wedding at Cana
\p \v 1 On the third day a wedding took place at Cana in Galilee. Jesus’ mother was there, \v 2 and Jesus and His disciples had also been invited to the wedding. \v 3 When \add the\add* wine ran out, Jesus’ mother said to Him, “They have no more wine.”
\p \v 4 \wj “Woman, what \add is that\add* to you and to Me?”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “My hour \add has\add* not yet come.”\wj*
\p \v 5 His mother said to the servants, “Do whatever He tells you.”
\p \v 6 Now six stone water jars had been set there for the Jewish \add rites of\add* purification. \add Each\add* could hold from twenty to thirty gallons.\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Greek \fqa two or three metretae\ft ; that is, approximately 20.8 to 31.2 gallons (78.8 to 118.1 liters)\f* \v 7 Jesus told \add the servants\add*, \wj “Fill the jars with water.”\wj*
\p So they filled them to \add the\add* brim.
\p \v 8 \wj “Now draw \add some\add* out,”\wj* He said, \wj “and take \add it\add* to the master of the banquet.”\wj*
\p They \add did so\add*, \v 9 and the master of the banquet tasted the water that had been turned into wine. He did not know where it was \add from\add*, but the servants who had drawn the water knew. Then \add he\add* called the bridegroom \add aside\add* \v 10 and said, “Everyone serves the fine wine first, and then the cheap \add wine\add* after \add the guests\add* are drunk. \add But\add* you have saved the fine wine until now!”
\p \v 11 Jesus performed this, the first of His signs, at Cana in Galilee. He thus revealed His glory, and His disciples believed in Him.
\s1 Jesus Cleanses the Temple
\r (\ref Matthew 21:12–17|MAT 21:12-17\ref*; \ref Mark 11:15–19|MRK 11:15-19\ref*; \ref Luke 19:45–48|LUK 19:45-48\ref*)
\p \v 12 After this, He went down to Capernaum \add with\add* His mother and brothers and His disciples, and they stayed there a few days.
\p \v 13 When the Jewish Passover was near, Jesus went up to Jerusalem. \v 14 In the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\ft ; also in verse 15\f* He found \add men\add* selling cattle, sheep, and doves, and money changers seated \add at their tables\add*. \v 15 \add So\add* He made a whip out of cords \add and\add* drove all from the temple \add courts\add*, both sheep and cattle. He poured out the coins of the money changers and overturned \add their\add* tables. \v 16 To those selling doves He said, \wj “Get these out of here! How dare you turn My Father’s house \add into\add* a marketplace!”\wj*
\p \v 17 His disciples remembered that it is written: “Zeal for Your house will consume Me.”\f + \fr 2:17 \ref Psalm 69:9|PSA 69:9\ref*\f*
\p \v 18 On account of this, the Jews demanded, “What sign can You show us to prove Your  \add authority \add* to do these things?”
\p \v 19 Jesus answered, \wj “Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up again.”\wj*
\p \v 20 “This temple \add took\add* forty-six years to build,” the Jews replied, “and You are going to raise it up in three days?”
\p \v 21 But \add Jesus\add* was speaking about the temple \add of\add* His body. \v 22 After He was raised from \add the\add* dead, His disciples remembered that He had said this. Then they believed the Scripture and the word that Jesus had spoken.
\p \v 23 While He was in Jerusalem at the Passover Feast, many people saw \add the\add* signs He was doing \add and\add* believed in His name. \v 24 But Jesus \add did\add* not entrust Himself to them, for He knew \add them\add* all. \v 25 He \add did\add* not need any testimony about man, for He knew what was in \add a\add* man.
\c 3
\s1 Jesus and Nicodemus
\r (\ref Genesis 22:1–10|GEN 22:1-10\ref*; \ref Romans 5:6–11|ROM 5:6-11\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now there was a man of the Pharisees named Nicodemus, a leader of the Jews. \v 2 He came to \add Jesus\add* at night and said, “Rabbi, we know that \add You are\add* a teacher who has come from God. For no one could perform \add the\add* signs You are doing if God were not with him.”
\p \v 3 Jesus replied, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, no one can see the kingdom of God unless he is born again.\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Or \fqa born from above\ft ; also in verse 7.\f*”\wj*
\p \v 4 “How can a man be born when he is old?” Nicodemus asked. “Can he enter his mother’s womb a second time to be born?”
\p \v 5 Jesus answered, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, no one can enter the kingdom of God unless he is born of water and \add the\add* Spirit.\wj* \v 6 \wj Flesh is born of flesh, \add but\add* spirit is born of the Spirit.\wj* \v 7 \wj Do not be amazed that I said, ‘You\f + \fr 3:7 \ft The Greek word for \fqa you \ft is plural; also in verse 12.\f* must be born again.’\wj* \v 8 \wj The wind blows where it wishes. You hear its sound, but you do not know where it comes \add from or\add* where it is going. So it is with everyone born of the Spirit.”\wj*
\p \v 9 “How can this be?” Nicodemus asked.
\p \v 10 \wj “You are Israel’s teacher,”\wj* said Jesus, \wj “and you do not understand these things?\wj* \v 11 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, we speak of what we know, and we testify to what we have seen, and yet you people do not accept our testimony.\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj If I have told you \add about\add* earthly things and you do not believe, how will you believe if I tell you \add about\add* heavenly things?\wj* \v 13 \wj No one has ascended into heaven except the \add One who\add* descended from heaven— the Son of Man.\f + \fr 3:13 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa who is in heaven\ft .\f*\wj* \v 14 \wj Just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up,\wj* \v 15 \wj that everyone who believes in Him may have eternal life.\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Or \fqa everyone who believes may have eternal life in Him.\f*\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj For God so loved the world that He gave \add His\add* one and only\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or \ft only begotten \ft or \fqa unique\ft ; also in verse 18\f* Son, that everyone who believes in Him shall not perish but have eternal life.\wj* \v 17 \wj For God \add did\add* not send His Son into the world to condemn the world, but to save the world through Him.\wj* \v 18 \wj Whoever believes in Him is not condemned, but whoever does not believe has already been condemned, because he has not believed in the name of God’s one and only Son.\wj*
\p \v 19 \wj And this is the verdict: The Light has come into the world, but men loved the darkness rather than the Light because their deeds were evil.\wj* \v 20 \wj Everyone who does evil hates the Light, and \add does\add* not come into the Light for fear that his deeds will be exposed.\wj* \v 21 \wj But whoever practices the truth comes into the Light, so that it may be seen clearly that \add what\add* he has done has been accomplished in God.”\f + \fr 3:21 \ft Some translators close this quotation after verse 15.\f*\wj*
\s1 John’s Testimony about Jesus
\p \v 22 After this, Jesus and His disciples went into the Judean countryside, \add where\add* He spent some time with them and baptized.
\p \v 23 Now John was also baptizing at Aenon near Salim, because \add the\add* water was plentiful there, and \add people\add* kept coming to be baptized. \v 24 (For John \add had\add* not yet been thrown into prison.)
\p \v 25 Then a dispute arose between John’s disciples \add and a certain\add* Jew\f + \fr 3:25 \ft TR \fqa and the Jews\f* over \add the issue of\add* ceremonial washing. \v 26 So \add John’s disciples\add* came to \add him\add* and said, “Look, Rabbi, the \add One who\add* was with you beyond the Jordan, the One you testified about — He is baptizing, and everyone is going to Him.”
\p \v 27 John replied, “A man can receive only what is given him from heaven. \v 28 You yourselves can testify that I said, ‘I am not the Christ, but am sent ahead of Him.’ \v 29 The bride belongs to \add the\add* bridegroom. The friend of the bridegroom stands and listens for him, \add and\add* is overjoyed \add to hear\add* the bridegroom’s voice. That joy \add is\add* mine, \add and\add* it is now complete. \v 30 He must increase; I \add must\add* decrease.
\p \v 31 The \add One who\add* comes from above is above all. The \add one who\add* is from the earth belongs to the earth and speaks \add as one\add* from the earth. The \add One who\add* comes from heaven is above all.\f + \fr 3:31 \ft Tischendorf \fqa The One comes from heaven.\f* \v 32 He testifies to what He has seen and heard, yet no one accepts His testimony. \v 33 Whoever accepts His testimony has certified that God is truthful. \v 34 For the \add One\add* whom God has sent speaks the words of God, for \add God\add* gives the Spirit without limit.
\p \v 35 The Father loves the Son and has placed all things in His hands. \v 36 Whoever believes in the Son has eternal life. Whoever rejects the Son will not see life. Instead, the wrath of God remains on him.”\f + \fr 3:36 \ft Some translators close this quotation after verse 30.\f*
\c 4
\s1 Jesus and the Samaritan Woman
\p \v 1 When Jesus realized that the Pharisees were aware \add He\add*\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Literally \fqa When therefore Jesus knew that the Pharisees had heard that Jesus\ft ; NE, WH, BYZ, and TR \fqa When therefore the Lord knew that the Pharisees had heard that Jesus\f* was gaining and baptizing more disciples than John \v 2 (although \add it was\add* not Jesus who baptized, but His disciples), \v 3 He left Judea and returned to Galilee.
\p \v 4 Now He had to pass through Samaria. \v 5 So He came to a town of Samaria called Sychar, near the plot of ground that Jacob had given to his son Joseph. \v 6 Since Jacob’s well was there, Jesus, weary from \add His\add* journey, sat down by the well. It was about \add the\add* sixth hour.\f + \fr 4:6 \ft That is, about noon\f*
\p \v 7 \add When\add* a Samaritan woman came to draw water, Jesus said to her, \wj “Give Me a drink.”\wj* \v 8 (His disciples had gone into the town to buy food.)
\p \v 9 “You are a Jew,” said the woman. “How \add can\add* You ask for a drink from me, a Samaritan woman?” (For Jews do not associate with Samaritans.)\f + \fr 4:9 \ft Tischendorf does not include this sentence.\f*
\p \v 10 Jesus answered, \wj “If you knew the gift of God and who is asking you \add for\add* a drink, you would have asked Him, and He would have given you living water.”\wj*
\p \v 11 “Sir,” the woman replied, “You have nothing to draw with and the well is deep. Where then will You get \add this\add* living water? \v 12 Are You greater than our father Jacob, who gave us \add this\add* well and drank from it himself, \add as did\add* his sons and his livestock?”
\p \v 13 Jesus said to her, \wj “Everyone who drinks this water will be thirsty again.\wj* \v 14 \wj But whoever drinks the water I give him will never thirst. Indeed, the water I give him will become in him a fount of water springing up to eternal life.”\wj*
\p \v 15 The woman said to Him, “Sir, give me this water so that I will not get thirsty \add and\add* have to keep coming here to draw \add water\add*.”
\p \v 16 \add Jesus\add* told her, \wj “Go, call your husband and come \add back\add*.”\wj*
\p \v 17 “I have no husband,” the woman replied.
\p Jesus said to her, \wj “You are correct \add to say\add* that you have no husband.\wj* \v 18 \wj In fact, you have had five husbands, and the man you now have is not your husband. You have spoken truthfully.”\wj*
\p \v 19 “Sir,” the woman said, “I see that You are a prophet. \v 20 Our fathers worshiped on this mountain, but you  \add Jews \add* say that the place where \add one\add* must worship is in Jerusalem.”
\p \v 21 \wj “Believe Me, woman,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “a time is coming when you will worship the Father neither on this mountain nor in Jerusalem.\wj* \v 22 \wj You worship what you do not know; we worship what we do know, for salvation is from the Jews.\wj* \v 23 \wj But a time is coming and \add has\add* now come when the true worshipers will worship the Father in spirit and in truth, for the Father is seeking such as these to worship Him.\wj* \v 24 \wj God \add is\add* Spirit, and \add His\add* worshipers must worship Him in spirit and \add in\add* truth.”\wj*
\p \v 25 The woman said, “I know that Messiah” (called Christ) “is coming. When He comes, He will explain everything to us.”
\p \v 26 Jesus answered, \wj “I who speak to you am \add He\add*.”\wj*
\s1 The Disciples Return and Marvel
\p \v 27 Just then His disciples returned and were surprised that He was speaking with a woman. But no one asked \add Him\add*, “What do You want \add from her\add*?” or “Why are You talking with her?”
\p \v 28 Then the woman left her water jar, went back into the town, and said to the people, \v 29 “Come, see a man who told me everything I ever did. Could this be the Christ?” \v 30 \add So\add* they left the town and made their way toward \add Jesus\add*.
\p \v 31 Meanwhile the disciples urged Him, “Rabbi, eat \add something\add*.”
\p \v 32 But He told them, \wj “I have food to eat that you know nothing \add about\add*.”\wj*
\p \v 33 So the disciples asked one another, “\add Could\add* someone have brought Him food?”
\p \v 34 Jesus explained, \wj “My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me and to finish His work.\wj* \v 35 \wj \add Do\add* you not say, ‘There are still four months \add until\add* the harvest’? I tell you, lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are ripe\f + \fr 4:35 \ft Literally \fqa white\f* for harvest.\wj*
\p \v 36 \wj \add Already\add* the reaper draws \add his\add* wages and gathers a crop for eternal life, so that the sower and the reaper may rejoice together.\wj* \v 37 \wj For in this case the saying ‘One sows and another reaps’ is true.\wj* \v 38 \wj I sent you to reap what you have not worked for; others have done the hard work, and \add now\add* you have taken up their labor.”\wj*
\s1 Many Samaritans Believe
\p \v 39 Many of the Samaritans from that town believed in \add Jesus\add* because of the woman’s testimony, “He told me everything I ever did.” \v 40 So when the Samaritans came to Him, they asked Him to stay with them, and He stayed two days.
\p \v 41 And many more believed because of His message. \v 42 They said to the woman, “We now believe not only because of your words; we have heard for ourselves, and we know that this \add man\add* truly is the Savior of the world.”
\s1 Jesus Heals the Official’s Son
\r (\ref Matthew 8:5–13|MAT 8:5-13\ref*; \ref Luke 7:1–10|LUK 7:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 43 After two days, \add Jesus\add* left for Galilee. \v 44 Now \add He\add* Himself had testified that a prophet has no honor in \add his\add* own hometown. \v 45 Yet when He arrived, the Galileans welcomed Him. They had seen all the great things He had done in Jerusalem at the feast, for they had gone \add there\add* as well.
\p \v 46 So once again He came to Cana in Galilee, where He had turned the water \add into\add* wine. And there was a royal official whose son lay sick at Capernaum. \v 47 \add When\add* he heard that Jesus had come from Judea to Galilee, he went and begged Him to come down and heal his son, who was about to die.
\p \v 48 Jesus said to him, \wj “Unless you people see signs and wonders, you will never believe.”\wj*
\p \v 49 “Sir,” the official said, “come down before my child dies.”
\p \v 50 \wj “Go,”\wj* said Jesus. \wj “Your son will live.”\wj*
\p The man took Jesus at His word and departed. \v 51 And while he \add was\add* still on the way, his servants met him with the news that his boy was alive.
\p \v 52 So he inquired as to the hour when \add his son\add* had recovered, and they told him, “The fever left him yesterday \add at the\add* seventh hour.\f + \fr 4:52 \ft That is, one in the afternoon\f*”
\p \v 53 Then the father realized that this was the very hour in which Jesus had told him, \wj “Your son will live.”\wj* And he and all his household believed.
\p \v 54 This \add was\add* now \add the\add* second sign \add that\add* Jesus performed after coming from Judea into Galilee.
\c 5
\s1 The Pool of Bethesda
\p \v 1 Some time later there was a feast of the Jews, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem.
\p \v 2 Now there is in Jerusalem near the Sheep Gate a pool with five covered colonnades, which in Hebrew\f + \fr 5:2 \ft Or \fqa in Aramaic\f* is called Bethesda.\f + \fr 5:2 \ft NA, NE, and WH \fqa Bethzatha\f* \v 3 On these  \add walkways \add*\f + \fr 5:3 \ft Literally \fqa In these\f* lay a great number of the sick, \add the\add* blind, \add the\add* lame, \add and the\add* paralyzed.\f + \fr 5:3 \ft NE, BYZ, and TR include \fqa awaiting the moving of the waters. \fv 4\fv*For from time to time an angel descended into the pool and stirred the water. As soon as it was stirred, the first to enter the pool would be healed of his disease.\f*
\p \v 5 One man there had been \add an\add* invalid \add for\add* thirty-eight years. \v 6 When Jesus saw him lying \add there\add* and realized that he had spent a long time \add in this condition\add*, He asked him, \wj “Do you want to get well?”\wj*
\p \v 7 “Sir,” the invalid replied, “I have no one to help me into the pool when the water is stirred. While I am on my way, someone else goes in before me.”
\p \v 8 Then Jesus told him, \wj “Get up, pick up your mat, and walk.”\wj*
\p \v 9 Immediately the man was made well, and he picked up his mat and began to walk.
\p Now this happened on the Sabbath day, \v 10 so the Jews said to the \add man\add* who had been healed, “This is \add the\add* Sabbath! It is unlawful for you to carry your mat.”
\p \v 11 But he answered, “The \add man who\add* made me well told me, \wj ‘Pick up your mat and walk.’\wj*”
\p \v 12 “Who is \add this\add* man who told you to pick \add it\add* up and walk?” they asked.
\p \v 13 But the \add man who\add* was healed \add did\add* not know who it was, for Jesus had slipped away \add while the\add* crowd was there.
\p \v 14 Afterward, Jesus found \add the man\add* at the temple and said to him, \wj “See, you have been made well. Stop sinning, or something worse may happen to you.”\wj*
\p \v 15 \add And\add* the man went away and told the Jews that it was Jesus who had made him well.
\s1 The Father and the Son
\p \v 16 Now because \add Jesus\add* was doing these things on \add the\add* Sabbath, the Jews began to persecute \add Him\add*. \v 17 But Jesus answered them, \wj “To this very day My Father is at His work, and I too am working.”\wj*
\p \v 18 Because of this, the Jews tried all the harder to kill Him. Not only was He breaking the Sabbath, but He was even calling God His own Father, making Himself equal with God.
\p \v 19 So Jesus replied, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, the Son can do nothing by Himself, unless He sees the Father doing \add it\add*. For whatever \add the Father\add* does, the Son also does.\wj* \v 20 \wj The Father loves the Son and shows Him all He does. And to your amazement, He will show Him even greater works than these.\wj* \v 21 \wj For just as the Father raises the dead and gives \add them\add* life, so also the Son gives life to whom He wishes.\wj*
\p \v 22 \wj Furthermore, the Father judges no one, but has assigned all judgment to the Son,\wj* \v 23 \wj so that all may honor the Son just as they honor the Father. Whoever does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent Him.\wj*
\p \v 24 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, whoever hears My word and believes Him who sent Me has eternal life and \add will\add* not come under judgment. Indeed, he has crossed over from death to life.\wj*
\p \v 25 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, \add the\add* hour is coming and has now \add come\add* when the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God, and those who hear will live.\wj* \v 26 \wj For as the Father has life in Himself, so also He has granted the Son to have life in Himself.\wj* \v 27 \wj And He has given Him authority to execute judgment, because He is \add the\add* Son of Man.\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj \add Do\add* not be amazed at this, for \add the\add* hour is coming when all who \add are\add* in \add their\add* graves will hear His voice\wj* \v 29 \wj and come out — those who have done good to \add the\add* resurrection of life, and those who have done evil to \add the\add* resurrection of judgment.\f + \fr 5:29 \ft See \ref Daniel 12:2|DAN 12:2\ref*.\f*\wj*
\p \v 30 \wj I can do nothing by Myself; I judge only as I hear. And My judgment is just, because I do not seek My \add own\add* will, but the will of \add Him who\add* sent Me.\wj*
\s1 Testimonies about Jesus
\p \v 31 \wj If I testify about Myself, My testimony is not valid.\wj* \v 32 \wj There is another who testifies about Me, and I know that \add His\add* testimony about Me is valid.\wj*
\p \v 33 \wj You have sent to John, and he has testified to the truth.\wj* \v 34 \wj \add Even though\add* I \add do\add* not accept human testimony, I say these things so that you may be saved.\wj*
\p \v 35 \wj \add John\add* was a lamp that burned and gave light, and you were willing for a season to bask in his light.\wj* \v 36 \wj But I have testimony more substantial than that of John. For the works that the Father has given Me to accomplish — the very works I am doing — testify about Me that the Father has sent Me.\wj* \v 37 \wj And \add the\add* Father who sent Me has Himself testified about Me. You have never heard His voice nor seen His form,\wj* \v 38 \wj \add nor does\add* His word abide in you, because you \add do\add* not believe the \add One\add* He sent.\wj*
\s1 The Witness of Scripture
\r (\ref Luke 16:19–31|LUK 16:19-31\ref*)
\p \v 39 \wj You pore over the Scriptures because you presume that by them you possess eternal life. These are the \add very words that\add* testify about Me,\wj* \v 40 \wj yet you refuse to come to Me to have life.\wj*
\p \v 41 \wj I do not accept glory from men,\wj* \v 42 \wj but I know you, that you do not have the love of God within you.\wj* \v 43 \wj I have come in My Father’s name, and you have not received Me; \add but\add* if someone else comes in \add his\add* own name, you will receive him.\wj* \v 44 \wj How can you believe if you accept glory from one another, yet do not seek the glory that \add comes\add* from the only God?\wj*
\p \v 45 \wj Do not think that I will accuse you before the Father. Your accuser is Moses, in whom you have put your hope.\wj* \v 46 \wj If you had believed Moses, you would believe Me, because he wrote about Me.\wj* \v 47 \wj But since you do not believe what he wrote, how will you believe what I say?”\wj*
\c 6
\s1 The Feeding of the Five Thousand
\r (\ref Matthew 14:13–21|MAT 14:13-21\ref*; \ref Mark 6:30–44|MRK 6:30-44\ref*; \ref Luke 9:10–17|LUK 9:10-17\ref*)
\p \v 1 After this, Jesus crossed to the other side of the Sea of Galilee (that \add is\add*, \add the Sea\add* of Tiberias). \v 2 A large crowd followed Him because they saw the signs He was performing on the sick. \v 3 Then Jesus went up on the mountain and sat down with His disciples.
\p \v 4 Now the Jewish Feast of the Passover was near. \v 5 \add When\add* Jesus looked up and saw a large crowd coming toward Him, He said to Philip, \wj “Where can we buy bread for these \add people\add* to eat?”\wj* \v 6 But He was asking this to test him, for He knew what He was about to do.
\p \v 7 Philip answered, “Two hundred denarii\f + \fr 6:7 \ft A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see \ref Matthew 20:2|MAT 20:2\ref*.\f* would not \add buy\add* enough bread for each \add of them\add* to have a small \add piece\add*.”
\p \v 8 One of His disciples, Andrew, Simon Peter’s brother, said to Him, \v 9 “Here is a boy \add with\add* five barley loaves and two small fish. But what \add difference \add* will these make among so many?”
\p \v 10 \wj “Have the \add people\add* sit down,”\wj* Jesus said. Now there was plenty of grass in \add that\add* place, so the men sat down, about five thousand \add of them\add*.
\p \v 11 Then Jesus took the loaves and the fish, gave thanks, \add and\add* distributed to those who were seated as much as they wanted.
\p \v 12 And when everyone was full, He said to His disciples, \wj “Gather the pieces that are left over, so that nothing will be wasted.”\wj*
\p \v 13 So they collected \add them\add* and filled twelve baskets with the pieces of the five barley loaves left over by those who had eaten.
\p \v 14 \add When\add* the people saw the sign that \add Jesus\add* had performed,\f + \fr 6:14 \ft SBL, NA, NE, and WH \fqa He had performed\f* they began to say, “Truly this is the Prophet who is to come into the world.”
\p \v 15 Then Jesus, realizing that they were about to come and make Him king by force, withdrew again to a mountain by Himself.
\s1 Jesus Walks on Water
\r (\ref Matthew 14:22–33|MAT 14:22-33\ref*; \ref Mark 6:45–52|MRK 6:45-52\ref*)
\p \v 16 When evening came, His disciples went down to the sea, \v 17 got into a boat, \add and\add* started across the sea to Capernaum. It was already dark, and Jesus had not yet gone out to them. \v 18 A strong wind was blowing, and the sea grew agitated.
\p \v 19 \add When\add* they had rowed about three or four miles,\f + \fr 6:19 \ft Greek \fqa about twenty-five or thirty stadia\ft ; that is, approximately 2.87 to 3.45 miles (4.62 to 5.55 kilometers)\f* they saw Jesus approaching the boat, walking on the sea — and they were terrified. \v 20 But \add Jesus\add* spoke up: \wj “It is I; \add do\add* not be afraid.”\wj* \v 21 Then they were willing to take Him into the boat, and at once the boat reached the shore where they were heading.
\s1 Jesus the Bread of Life
\p \v 22 The next day, the crowd that had remained on the other side of the sea realized that only one boat had been there, and that Jesus had not boarded \add it with\add* His disciples, but \add they\add* had gone away alone. \v 23 However, \add some\add* boats from Tiberias landed near the place where \add the people\add* had eaten the bread \add after\add* the Lord had given thanks. \v 24 So when the crowd saw that neither Jesus nor His disciples were there, they got into the boats and went to Capernaum to look for \add Him\add*. \v 25 \add When\add* they found Him on the other side of the sea, they asked Him, “Rabbi, when did You get here?”
\p \v 26 Jesus replied, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, \add it is\add* not because you saw \add these\add* signs \add that\add* you are looking for Me, but because you ate the loaves and had your fill.\wj* \v 27 \wj \add Do\add* not work \add for\add* food that perishes, but \add for\add* food that endures to eternal life, which the Son of Man will give you. For on Him God the Father has placed His seal of approval.”\wj*
\p \v 28 Then they inquired, “What must we do to perform the works of God?”
\p \v 29 Jesus replied, \wj “The work of God is this: to believe in the \add One\add* He has sent.”\wj*
\p \v 30 So they asked Him, “What sign then \add will\add* You perform, so that we may see \add it\add* and believe You? What will You do? \v 31 Our fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, as it is written: ‘He gave them bread from heaven to eat.’\f + \fr 6:31 \ref Psalm 78:24|PSA 78:24\ref*; see also Exodus 16:4.\f*”
\p \v 32 Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, \add it was\add* not Moses \add who\add* gave you the bread from heaven, but \add it is\add* My Father \add who\add* gives you the true bread from heaven.\wj* \v 33 \wj For the bread of God is He who comes down from heaven and gives life to the world.”\wj*
\p \v 34 “Sir,” they said, “give us this bread at all times.”
\p \v 35 Jesus answered, \wj “I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to Me will never hunger, and whoever believes in Me will never thirst.\wj* \v 36 \wj But \add as\add* I stated, you have seen Me and still \add you do\add* not believe.\wj*
\p \v 37 \wj Everyone the Father gives Me will come to Me, and the \add one who\add* comes to Me I will never drive away.\wj* \v 38 \wj For I have come down from heaven, not to do My \add own\add* will, but \add to do\add* the will of Him who sent Me.\wj*
\p \v 39 \wj And this is the will of Him who sent Me, that I shall lose none of those He has given Me, but raise them up at the last day.\wj* \v 40 \wj For it is My Father’s will that everyone who looks to the Son and believes in Him shall have eternal life, and I will raise him up at the last day.”\wj*
\p \v 41 At this, the Jews began to grumble about \add Jesus\add* because He had said, \wj “I am the bread that came down from heaven.”\wj* \v 42 They were asking, “Is this not Jesus, the son of Joseph, whose father and mother we know? How then can He say, \wj ‘I have come down from heaven’\wj*?”
\p \v 43 \wj “Stop grumbling among yourselves,”\wj* Jesus replied. \v 44 \wj “No one can come to Me unless the Father who sent Me draws him, and I will raise him up at the last day.\wj* \v 45 \wj It is written in the Prophets: ‘And they will all be taught by God.’\f + \fr 6:45 \ref Isaiah 54:13|ISA 54:13\ref*\f* Everyone who has heard the Father and learned from \add Him\add* comes to Me —\wj* \v 46 \wj not that anyone has seen the Father except the \add One who\add* is from God; \add only\add* He has seen the Father.\wj*
\p \v 47 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, he who believes has eternal life.\wj* \v 48 \wj I am the bread of life.\wj* \v 49 \wj Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, yet they died.\wj* \v 50 \wj This is the bread that comes down from heaven, so that anyone may eat of it and not die.\wj* \v 51 \wj I am the living bread that came down from heaven. If anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever. And \add this\add* bread, which I will give for the life of the world, is My flesh.”\wj*
\p \v 52 At this, the Jews began to argue among themselves, “How can this \add man\add* give us His flesh to eat?”
\p \v 53 So Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, unless you eat the flesh and drink the blood of the Son of Man, you have no life in you.\wj* \v 54 \wj Whoever eats My flesh and drinks My blood has eternal life, and I will raise him up at the last day.\wj* \v 55 \wj For My flesh is real food, and My blood is real drink.\wj*
\p \v 56 \wj Whoever eats My flesh and drinks My blood remains in Me, and I in him.\wj* \v 57 \wj Just as the living Father sent Me and I live because of the Father, so also the \add one who\add* feeds on Me will live because of Me.\wj* \v 58 \wj This is the bread that came down from heaven. Unlike \add your\add* fathers, \add who\add* ate \add the manna \add* and died, the \add one who\add* eats this bread will live forever.”\wj*
\s1 Many Disciples Turn Back
\r (\ref Matthew 8:18–22|MAT 8:18-22\ref*; \ref Luke 9:57–62|LUK 9:57-62\ref*; \ref Luke 14:25–33|LUK 14:25-33\ref*)
\p \v 59 \add Jesus\add* said this while teaching in \add the\add* synagogue in Capernaum. \v 60 On hearing \add it\add*, many of His disciples said, “This is a difficult teaching. Who can accept it?”
\p \v 61 Aware that His disciples were grumbling about this  \add teaching \add*, Jesus asked them, \wj “\add Does\add* this offend you?\wj* \v 62 \wj Then what \add will happen \add* if you see the Son of Man ascend to where He was before?\wj*
\p \v 63 \wj The Spirit gives life; the flesh profits nothing. The words I have spoken to you are spirit and they are life.\wj* \v 64 \wj However, there are some of you who do not believe.”\wj* (For Jesus had known from \add the\add* beginning which of them did not believe and who would betray Him.)
\p \v 65 Then \add Jesus\add* said, \wj “This is why I told you that no one can come to Me unless the Father has granted it to him.”\wj*
\p \v 66 From that \add time on\add* many of His disciples turned back and no longer walked with Him.
\s1 Peter’s Confession of Faith
\r (\ref Matthew 16:13–20|MAT 16:13-20\ref*; \ref Mark 8:27–30|MRK 8:27-30\ref*; \ref Luke 9:18–20|LUK 9:18-20\ref*)
\p \v 67 So Jesus asked the Twelve, \wj “Do you want to leave too?”\wj*
\p \v 68 Simon Peter replied, “Lord, to whom would we go? You have \add the\add* words of eternal life. \v 69 We believe and know that You are the Holy One of God.\f + \fr 6:69 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa You are the Christ, the Son of the living God\f*”
\p \v 70 Jesus answered them, \wj “\add Have\add* I not chosen you, the Twelve? Yet one of you is a devil!”\wj* \v 71 He was speaking about Judas, \add the son\add* of Simon Iscariot. For \add although Judas was\add* one of the Twelve, \add he was\add* later to betray \add Jesus\add*.
\c 7
\s1 Jesus Teaches at the Feast
\p \v 1 After this, Jesus traveled throughout Galilee. He did not want to travel in Judea, because the Jews \add there\add* were trying to kill Him. \v 2 However, the Jewish Feast of Tabernacles\f + \fr 7:2 \ft That is, Sukkot, the autumn feast of pilgrimage to Jerusalem; also translated as \fqa the Feast of Booths \ft or \fqa the Feast of Shelters \ft and originally called \fqa the Feast of Ingathering\ft (see \ref Exodus 23:16|EXO 23:16\ref* and Exodus 34:22).\f* was near. \v 3 So \add Jesus’\add* brothers said to Him, “Leave here and go to Judea, so that Your disciples \add there\add* may see \add the\add* works You are doing. \v 4 For no one \add who\add* wants to be \add known\add* publicly acts in secret. Since You are doing these things, show Yourself to the world.” \v 5 For \add even\add* His \add own\add* brothers did \add not\add* believe in Him.
\p \v 6 Therefore Jesus told them, \wj “Although your time is always at hand, My time \add has\add* not yet come.\wj* \v 7 \wj The world cannot hate you, but it hates Me, because I testify that its works are evil.\wj* \v 8 \wj Go up to the feast on your own. I \add am\add* not\f + \fr 7:8 \ft NE, WH, BYZ, and TR \fqa I am not yet\f* going up to this feast, because My time \add has\add* not yet come.”\wj*
\p \v 9 Having said \add this\add*, \add Jesus\add* remained in Galilee. \v 10 But \add after\add* His brothers had gone up to the feast, He also went — not publicly, but in secret.
\p \v 11 So the Jews were looking for Him at the feast and asking, “Where is He?”
\p \v 12 Many in the crowds were whispering about Him. Some said, “He is a good \add man\add*.”
\p But others replied, “No, He deceives the people.”
\p \v 13 Yet no one would speak publicly about Him for fear of the Jews.
\p \v 14 About halfway through the feast, Jesus went up to the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\ft ; also in verse 28\f* and began to teach. \v 15 The Jews were amazed \add and\add* asked, “How \add did\add* this \add man\add* attain \add such\add* learning without having studied?”
\p \v 16 \wj “My teaching is not My own,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “\add It comes\add* from Him who sent Me.\wj* \v 17 \wj If anyone desires to do His will, he will know whether \add My\add* teaching is from God or \add whether\add* I speak on My own.\wj* \v 18 \wj He who speaks on his own \add authority\add* seeks \add his\add* own glory, but He who seeks the glory of the \add One who\add* sent Him is a man of truth; in Him there is no falsehood.\wj* \v 19 \wj \add Has\add* not Moses given you the law? Yet not one of you keeps \add it\add*. Why are you trying to kill Me?”\wj*
\p \v 20 “You have a demon,” the crowd replied. “Who is trying to kill You?”
\p \v 21 Jesus answered them, \wj “I did one miracle, and you are all amazed.\wj* \v 22 \wj \add But\add* because Moses gave you circumcision, you circumcise a boy on \add the\add* Sabbath (not that it is from Moses, but from the patriarchs.)\wj* \v 23 \wj If a boy can be circumcised on \add the\add* Sabbath so that the law of Moses \add will\add* not be broken, \add why\add* are you angry with Me for making \add the\add* whole man well on the Sabbath?\wj* \v 24 \wj Stop judging by outward appearances, \add and start\add* judging justly.”\wj*
\s1 Is Jesus the Christ?
\p \v 25 Then some of the \add people\add* of Jerusalem began to say, “Isn’t this \add the man\add* they are trying to kill? \v 26 Yet here He is, speaking publicly, and they are not saying \add anything\add* to Him. \add Have\add* the rulers truly recognized that this is the Christ? \v 27 But we know where this \add man\add* is \add from\add*. When the Christ comes, no one will know where He is \add from\add*.”
\p \v 28 Then Jesus, still teaching in the temple \add courts\add*, cried out, \wj “You know Me, and you know where I am \add from\add*. I have not come of My own accord, but He who sent Me is true. You \add do\add* not know Him,\wj* \v 29 \wj but I know Him, because I am from Him and He sent Me.”\wj*
\p \v 30 So they tried to seize Him, but no one laid a hand on Him, because His hour \add had\add* not yet come. \v 31 Many in the crowd, however, believed in Him and said, “When the Christ comes, will He perform more signs than this \add man\add*?”
\p \v 32 \add When\add* the Pharisees heard the crowd whispering these things about \add Jesus\add*, \add they\add* and the chief priests sent officers to arrest Him. \v 33 So Jesus said, \wj “I am with you only a little while longer, and then I am going to the \add One who\add* sent Me.\wj* \v 34 \wj You will look for Me, \add but\add* you will not find Me; and where I am, you cannot come.”\wj*
\p \v 35 At this, the Jews said to one another, “Where \add does\add* He intend to go that we will not find Him? Will He go where the \add Jews\add* are dispersed among the Greeks,\f + \fr 7:35 \ft Literally \fqa Will He go to the Diaspora among the Greeks\ft ; the Jewish people living outside the land of Israel since the Babylonian exile were referred to as \fqa the Diaspora\ft .\f* and teach the Greeks? \v 36 What does He mean by saying, \wj ‘You will look for Me, \add but\add* you will not find Me,’\wj* and, \wj ‘Where I am, you cannot come’\wj*?”
\s1 Living Water
\p \v 37 On the last \add and\add* greatest day of the feast, Jesus stood up and called out in a loud voice, \wj “If anyone is thirsty, let him come to Me and drink.\wj* \v 38 \wj Whoever believes in Me, as the Scripture has said: ‘Streams of living water will flow from \add within\add* him.’”\wj* \v 39 He was speaking about the Spirit, whom those who believed in Him were later to receive. For \add the\add* Spirit had not yet \add been given\add*,\f + \fr 7:39 \ft Literally \fqa the Spirit was not yet\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa the Holy Spirit was not yet\f* because Jesus \add had\add* not yet been glorified.
\s1 Division over Jesus
\p \v 40 On hearing these words, \add some\add* of the people said, “This is truly the Prophet.”
\p \v 41 Others declared, “This is the Christ.”
\p But still others asked, “\add How can\add* the Christ come from Galilee? \v 42 Doesn’t the Scripture say that the Christ will come from the line of David and from Bethlehem, the village where David lived?\f + \fr 7:42 \ft See \ref Micah 5:2|MIC 5:2\ref*.\f*”
\p \v 43 So there was division in the crowd because of \add Jesus\add*. \v 44 Some of them wanted to seize Him, but no one laid a hand on Him.
\s1 The Unbelief of the Jewish Leaders
\p \v 45 Then the officers returned to the chief priests and Pharisees, \add who\add* asked them, “Why didn’t you bring Him \add in\add*?”
\p \v 46 “Never \add has\add* anyone spoken like this man!” the officers answered.
\p \v 47 “\add Have\add* you also been deceived?” replied the Pharisees. \v 48 “\add Have\add* any of the rulers or Pharisees believed in Him? \v 49 But this crowd that does not know the law— they are under a curse.”
\p \v 50 Nicodemus, who had gone to \add Jesus\add* earlier \add and who himself\add* was one of them, asked, \v 51 “\add Does\add* our law convict a man without first hearing from him to determine what he has done?”
\p \v 52 “Aren’t you also from Galilee?” they replied. “Look into it, and you will see that no prophet comes out of Galilee.”\f + \fr 7:52 \ft Some early manuscripts do not include \ref John 7:53|JHN 7:53\ref* through John 8:11.\f*
\p \v 53 Then each went to his own home.
\c 8
\s1 The Woman Caught in Adultery
\p \v 1 But Jesus went to the Mount of Olives.
\p \v 2 Early in the morning He went \add back\add* into the temple \add courts\add*.\f + \fr 8:2 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\ft ; also in verse 20\f* All the people came to Him, and He sat down to teach them. \v 3 The scribes and Pharisees, however, brought to Him a woman caught in adultery. They made her stand before them \v 4 \add and\add* said, “Teacher, this woman was caught in the act of adultery. \v 5 In the Law Moses commanded us to stone such \add a woman\add*. So what do You say?”
\p \v 6 They said this to test Him, in order to have \add a basis\add* for accusing Him. But Jesus bent down \add and\add* began to write on the ground with \add His\add* finger.
\p \v 7 When they continued to question Him, He straightened up and said to them, \wj “\add Let\add* him who is without sin among you \add be\add* the first to cast a stone at her.”\wj* \v 8 And again He bent down \add and\add* wrote on the ground.
\p \v 9 When they heard this,\f + \fr 8:9 \ft NE, BYZ, and TR include \fqa and were convicted by their conscience,\f* they began to go away one by one, beginning with the older ones, until only Jesus was left, with the woman standing \add there\add*. \v 10 Then Jesus straightened up\f + \fr 8:10 \ft NE, BYZ, and TR include \fqa and saw no one but the woman\ft .\f* \add and\add* asked her, \wj “Woman, where are your accusers?\f + \fr 8:10 \ft WH and NA \fqa where are they\f* \add Has\add* no one condemned you?”\wj*
\p \v 11 “No one, Lord,” she answered.
\p \wj “Then neither do I condemn you,”\wj* Jesus declared. \wj “Now go and sin no more.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus the Light of the World
\r (\ref 1 John 1:5–10|1JN 1:5-10\ref*)
\p \v 12 Once again, Jesus spoke to \add the people and\add* said, \wj “I am the light of the world. Whoever follows Me will never walk in the darkness, but will have the light of life.”\wj*
\p \v 13 So the Pharisees said to Him, “You are testifying about Yourself; Your testimony is not valid.”
\p \v 14 Jesus replied, \wj “Even if I testify about Myself, My testimony is valid, because I know where I came \add from\add* and where I am going. But you \add do\add* not know where I came \add from\add* or where I am going.\wj* \v 15 \wj You judge according to the flesh; I judge no one.\wj* \v 16 \wj But even if I do judge, My judgment is true, because I am not alone; I \add am with\add* the Father \add who\add* sent Me.\f + \fr 8:16 \ft Literally \fqa but (it is) I and the Father who sent Me\ft ; NE \fqa but (it is) I and the One who sent Me\f*\wj* \v 17 \wj Even in your \add own\add* Law it is written that the testimony of two men is valid.\f + \fr 8:17 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 19:15|DEU 19:15\ref*.\f*\wj* \v 18 \wj I am \add One\add* who testifies about Myself, and the Father, \add who\add* sent Me, \add also\add* testifies about Me.”\wj*
\p \v 19 “Where is Your Father?” they asked Him.
\p \wj “You do not know Me \add or\add* My Father,”\wj* Jesus answered. \wj “If you knew Me, you would know My Father as well.”\wj*
\p \v 20 He spoke these words while teaching in the temple \add courts\add*, near the treasury. Yet no one seized Him, because His hour \add had\add* not yet come.
\p \v 21 Again He said to them, \wj “I am going away, and you will look for Me, \add but\add* you will die in your sin. Where I am going, you cannot come.”\wj*
\p \v 22 So the Jews began to ask, “Will He kill Himself, since He says, \wj ‘Where I am going, you cannot come’\wj*?”
\p \v 23 Then He told them, \wj “You are from below; I am from above. You are of this world; I am not of this world.\wj* \v 24 \wj That is why I told you that you would die in your sins. For unless you believe that I am \add He\add*, you will die in your sins.”\wj*
\p \v 25 “Who are You?” they asked.
\p \wj “Just what I have been telling you from the beginning,”\wj* Jesus replied. \v 26 \wj “I have much to say about you and \add much\add* to judge. But the \add One who\add* sent Me is truthful, and what I have heard from Him, I tell the world.”\wj*
\p \v 27 They did not understand that He was telling them \add about\add* the Father. \v 28 So Jesus said, \wj “When you have lifted up the Son of Man, then you will know that I am \add He\add*, and \add that\add* I do nothing on My own, but speak exactly what the Father has taught Me.\wj* \v 29 \wj He who sent Me is with Me. He has not left Me alone, because I always do what pleases Him.”\wj*
\s1 The Truth Will Set You Free
\r (\ref 2 John 1:4–6|2JN 4-6\ref*)
\p \v 30 As \add Jesus\add* spoke these things, many believed in Him. \v 31 So \add He\add* said to the Jews \add who\add* had believed Him, \wj “If you continue in My word, you are truly My disciples.\wj* \v 32 \wj Then you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.”\wj*
\p \v 33 “We are Abraham’s descendants,” they answered. “We have never been slaves to \add anyone\add*. How \add can\add* You say \add we\add* will be set free?”
\p \v 34 Jesus replied, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, everyone who sins is a slave to sin.\wj* \v 35 \wj A slave \add does\add* not remain in the house forever, \add but\add* a son remains forever.\wj* \v 36 \wj So if the Son sets you free, you will be free indeed.\wj*
\p \v 37 \wj I know you are Abraham’s descendants, but you are trying to kill Me because My word has no place within you.\wj* \v 38 \wj I speak of what I have seen in the presence of the Father, and you do what you have heard from \add your\add* father.”\wj*
\p \v 39 “Abraham is our father,” they replied.
\p \wj “If you were children of Abraham,”\wj* said Jesus, \wj “you would do the works of Abraham.\wj* \v 40 \wj But now you are trying to kill Me, a man who has told you the truth that I heard from God. Abraham never did such a thing.\wj* \v 41 \wj You are doing the works of your father.”\wj*
\p “We are not illegitimate children,” they declared. “Our only Father \add is\add* God \add Himself\add*.”
\p \v 42 Jesus said to them, \wj “If God were your Father, you would love Me, for I have come here from God. I have not come on My own, but He sent Me.\wj*
\p \v 43 \wj Why do you not understand what I am saying? \add It is\add* because you are unable to accept My message.\wj* \v 44 \wj You belong to \add your\add* father, the devil, and you want to carry out \add his\add* desires. He was a murderer from \add the\add* beginning, refusing to uphold the truth, because there is no truth in him. When he lies, he speaks \add his\add* native \add language\add*, because he is a liar and the father of \add lies\add*.\wj* \v 45 \wj But because I speak the truth, you do not believe Me!\wj*
\p \v 46 \wj Which of you can prove Me guilty of sin? If I speak \add the\add* truth, why \add do\add* you not believe Me?\wj* \v 47 \wj Whoever belongs to God hears the words of God. The reason you \add do\add* not hear is that you do not belong to God.”\wj*
\s1 Before Abraham Was Born, I Am
\p \v 48 The Jews answered Him, “Are we not right to say that You are a Samaritan and You have a demon?”
\p \v 49 \wj “I \add do\add* not have a demon,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “but I honor My Father, and you dishonor Me.\wj* \v 50 \wj I \add do\add* not seek My \add own\add* glory. There is One who seeks \add it\add*, and He is the Judge.\wj* \v 51 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, if anyone keeps My word, he will never see death.”\wj*
\p \v 52 “Now we know that You have a demon!” declared the Jews. “Abraham died, and so did the prophets, yet You say that anyone who keeps \add Your\add* word will never taste death. \v 53 Are You greater than our father Abraham? \add He\add* died, \add as did\add* the prophets. Who do You claim to be?”
\p \v 54 Jesus answered, \wj “If I glorify Myself, My glory means nothing. The \add One who\add* glorifies Me is My Father, \add of\add* whom you say, ‘He is our\f + \fr 8:54 \ft WH and TR \fqa Your\f* God.’\wj* \v 55 \wj You do not know Him, but I know Him. If I said I did not know Him, I would be a liar like you. But I do know Him, and I keep His word.\wj* \v 56 \wj Your father Abraham rejoiced that he would see My day. He saw \add it\add* and was glad.”\wj*
\p \v 57 Then the Jews said to Him, “You are not yet fifty years \add old\add*, and You have seen Abraham?”
\p \v 58 \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you,”\wj* Jesus declared, \wj “before Abraham was \add born\add*, I am!\f + \fr 8:58 \ft Literally \fqa before Abraham was, I am!\f*”\wj*
\p \v 59 At this, they picked up stones to throw at Him. But Jesus was hidden and went out of the temple area.\f + \fr 8:59 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa going through the midst of them, and so He passed by\ft .\f*
\c 9
\s1 Jesus Heals the Man Born Blind
\p \v 1 Now as \add Jesus\add* was passing by, He saw a man blind from birth, \v 2 and His disciples asked Him, “Rabbi, who sinned, this \add man\add* or his parents, that he was born blind?”
\p \v 3 Jesus answered, \wj “Neither this \add man\add* nor his parents sinned, but \add this happened\add* so that the works of God would be displayed in him.\wj* \v 4 \wj While it is daytime, we must do\f + \fr 9:4 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa I must do\f* the works of Him who sent Me. Night is coming, when no one can work.\wj* \v 5 \wj While I am in the world, I am \add the\add* light of the world.”\wj*
\p \v 6 When \add Jesus\add* had said \add this\add*, He spit on \add the\add* ground, made \add some\add* mud, and applied \add it\add* to \add the man’s\add* eyes. \v 7 Then He told him, \wj “Go, wash in the Pool of Siloam”\wj* (which means “Sent”). So \add the man\add* went and washed, and came \add back\add* seeing.
\p \v 8 At this, \add his\add* neighbors and those who had formerly seen him begging began to ask, “Isn’t this the \add man who\add* used to sit and beg?”
\p \v 9 Some claimed that he was, but others said, “No, he just looks like him.”
\p \add But the man\add* kept saying, “I am \add the one\add*.”
\p \v 10 “How then \add were\add* your eyes opened?” they asked.
\p \v 11 He answered, “The man they call Jesus made \add some\add* mud and anointed my eyes, and He told me to go to Siloam and wash. So I went and washed \add and\add* received my sight.”
\p \v 12 “Where is He?” they asked.
\p “I do not know,” he answered.
\s1 The Pharisees Investigate the Healing
\p \v 13 They brought to the Pharisees \add the man\add* who had been blind. \v 14 Now \add the\add* day on which Jesus had made the mud and opened his eyes was a Sabbath. \v 15 So the Pharisees also asked him how he had received his sight.
\p \add The man\add* answered, “He put mud on my eyes, and I washed, and \add now\add* I can see.”
\p \v 16 Because of this, some of the Pharisees said, “This man is not from God, for He does not keep the Sabbath.”
\p But others said, “How can a sinful man perform such signs?”
\p And there was division among them. \v 17 So once again they asked the \add man who had been\add* blind, “What \add do\add* you say about Him, since \add it was\add* your eyes He opened?”
\p “He is a prophet,” \add the man\add* replied.
\p \v 18 The Jews still \add did\add* not believe that \add the man\add* had been blind and had received his sight until they summoned \add his\add* parents \v 19 and asked, “Is this your son, \add the one\add* you say was born blind? So how \add is it that\add* he can now see?”
\p \v 20 His parents answered, “We know he is our son, and \add we know\add* he was born blind. \v 21 But how he can now see or who opened his eyes, we \add do\add* not know. Ask him. He is old enough to speak for himself.”
\p \v 22 His parents said this because they were afraid of the Jews. For the Jews had already determined that anyone who confessed \add Jesus as the\add* Christ would be put out of the synagogue. \v 23 That was why his parents said, “He is old enough. Ask him.”
\p \v 24 So a second time they called for the man who had been blind and said, “Give glory to God! We know that this man is a sinner.”
\p \v 25 He answered, “Whether He is a sinner I do not know. \add There is\add* one \add thing\add* I do know: I was blind, \add but\add* now I see!”
\p \v 26 “What did He do to you?” they asked. “How did He open your eyes?”
\p \v 27 He replied, “I already told you, and you did not listen. Why do you want to hear \add it\add* again? \add Do\add* you also want to become His disciples?”
\p \v 28 Then they heaped insults on him and said, “You are His disciple; we are disciples of Moses. \v 29 We know that God spoke to Moses, but we do not know where this \add man\add* is \add from\add*.”
\p \v 30 “That is remarkable indeed!” the man said. “You \add do\add* not know where He is \add from\add*, and yet He opened my eyes. \v 31 We know that God does not listen to sinners, but He does listen to the one who worships \add Him\add* and does His will. \v 32 Never before \add has\add* anyone heard of opening \add the\add* eyes of a man born blind. \v 33 If this \add man\add* were not from God, He could do no such thing.”
\p \v 34 They replied, “You were born in utter sin, and you are instructing us?” And they threw him out.
\s1 Spiritual Blindness
\p \v 35 \add When\add* Jesus heard that they had thrown him out, He found \add the man and\add* said, \wj “\add Do\add* you believe in the Son of Man\f + \fr 9:35 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the Son of God\f*?”\wj*
\p \v 36 “Who is He, Sir?” he replied. “\add Tell me\add* so that I may believe in Him.”
\p \v 37 \wj “You have already seen Him,”\wj* Jesus answered. \wj “He is the \add One\add* speaking with you.”\wj*
\p \v 38 “Lord, I believe,” he said. And he worshiped \add Jesus\add*.
\p \v 39 Then Jesus declared, \wj “For judgment I have come into this world, so that the blind may see and those who see may become blind.”\wj*\f + \fr 9:39 \ft Literally \fqa so that those not seeing may see and those seeing may become blind. \ft Some manuscripts do not include \fqa \fv 38\fv*“Lord, I believe.”... \fv 39\fv*Then Jesus declared\ft .\f*
\p \v 40 \add Some\add* of the Pharisees who were with Him heard this, and they asked Him, “Are we blind too?”
\p \v 41 \wj “If you were blind,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “you would not be guilty of sin. But since you claim \add you\add* can see, your guilt remains.”\wj*
\c 10
\s1 Jesus the Good Shepherd
\r (\ref Psalm 23:1–6|PSA 23:1-6\ref*; \ref Ezekiel 34:11–24|EZK 34:11-24\ref*)
\p \v 1 \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, whoever does not enter the sheepfold by the gate, but climbs in some other way, is a thief and a robber.\wj* \v 2 \wj But the \add one who\add* enters by the gate is \add the\add* shepherd of the sheep.\wj* \v 3 \wj The gatekeeper opens \add the gate\add* for him, and the sheep listen for his voice. He calls \add his\add* own sheep by name and leads them out.\wj*
\p \v 4 \wj When he has brought out all \add his\add* own, he goes on ahead of them, and \add his\add* sheep follow him because they know his voice.\wj* \v 5 \wj But they will never follow a stranger; in fact, they will flee from him because they do not recognize \add his\add* voice.”\wj*
\p \v 6 Jesus spoke to them \add using\add* this illustration, but they \add did\add* not understand what He was telling them. \v 7 So \add He\add* said to them again, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, I am the gate for the sheep.\wj* \v 8 \wj All who came before Me\f + \fr 10:8 \ft BYZ \fqa All who came\f* were thieves and robbers, but the sheep did not listen to them.\wj* \v 9 \wj I am the gate. If anyone enters through Me, he will be saved. He will come in and go out and find pasture.\wj* \v 10 \wj The thief comes only to steal and kill and destroy. I have come that they may have life, and have \add it\add* in all its fullness.\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down His life for the sheep.\wj* \v 12 \wj The hired hand is not \add the\add* shepherd, \add and\add* the sheep are not his own. \add When\add* he sees the wolf coming, he abandons the sheep and runs away. Then the wolf pounces on them and scatters \add the flock\add*.\wj* \v 13 \wj \add The man runs away \add* because he is a hired servant and is unconcerned for the sheep.\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj I am the good shepherd. I know My \add sheep\add* and My \add sheep\add* know Me,\wj* \v 15 \wj just as the Father knows Me and I know the Father. And I lay down My life for the sheep.\wj* \v 16 \wj I have other sheep that are not of this fold. I must bring them in as well, and they will listen to My voice. Then there will be one flock \add and\add* one shepherd.\wj*
\p \v 17 \wj The reason the Father loves Me \add is\add* that I lay down My life in order to take it up again.\wj* \v 18 \wj No one takes it from Me, but I lay it down of My own accord. I have authority to lay it down and authority to take it up again. This charge I have received from My Father.”\wj*
\p \v 19 Again there was division among the Jews because of \add Jesus’\add* message. \v 20 Many of them said, “He is demon-possessed and insane. Why would you listen to Him?”
\p \v 21 \add But\add* others replied, “These are not the words of \add a man\add* possessed by a demon. Can a demon open \add the\add* eyes of \add the\add* blind?”
\s1 Jesus at the Feast of Dedication
\p \v 22 At that time the Feast of Dedication\f + \fr 10:22 \ft That is, Hanukkah, the historic celebration of the Maccabean Revolt and rededication of the temple\f* took place in Jerusalem. It was winter, \v 23 and Jesus was walking in the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 10:23 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\f* in Solomon’s Colonnade. \v 24 So the Jews gathered around Him and demanded, “How long will You keep us in suspense? If You are the Christ, tell us plainly.”
\p \v 25 \wj “I already told you,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “but you did not believe. The works I do in My Father’s name testify on My behalf.\wj* \v 26 \wj But because you are not My sheep, you refuse to believe.\wj* \v 27 \wj My sheep listen to My voice; I know them, and they follow Me.\wj* \v 28 \wj I give them eternal life, and they will never perish. No one can snatch them out of My hand.\wj* \v 29 \wj My Father who has given \add them\add* to Me is greater than all. No one can snatch \add them\add* out of \add My\add* Father’s hand.\wj* \v 30 \wj I and the Father are one.”\wj*
\p \v 31 At this, the Jews again picked up stones to stone Him. \v 32 \add But\add* Jesus responded, \wj “I have shown you many good works from the Father. For which of these do you stone Me?”\wj*
\p \v 33 “We are not stoning You for \add any\add* good work,” said the Jews, “but for blasphemy, because You, who are a man, make Yourself out to be God.”
\p \v 34 Jesus replied, \wj “Is it not written in your Law: ‘I have said you are gods’\f + \fr 10:34 \ref Psalm 82:6|PSA 82:6\ref*\f*?\wj* \v 35 \wj If he called them gods to whom the word of God came — and the Scripture cannot be broken —\wj* \v 36 \wj \add then what about the One \add* whom the Father sanctified and sent into the world? How then \add can\add* you accuse \add Me\add* of blasphemy for stating that I am \add the\add* Son of God?\wj*
\p \v 37 \wj If I am not doing the works of My Father, \add then do\add* not believe Me.\wj* \v 38 \wj But if I am doing \add them\add*, even though you do not believe Me, believe the works \add themselves\add*, so that you may know and understand that the Father \add is\add* in Me, and I am in the Father.”\wj*
\p \v 39 At this, they tried again to seize Him, but He escaped their grasp.
\s1 John’s Testimony Confirmed
\p \v 40 Then \add Jesus\add* went back across the Jordan to the place where John had first been baptizing, and He stayed there. \v 41 Many came to Him and said, “Although John never performed a sign, everything \add he\add* said about this \add man\add* was true.” \v 42 And many in that place believed in \add Jesus\add*.
\c 11
\s1 The Death of Lazarus
\p \v 1 At this time a \add man named\add* Lazarus was sick. \add He lived in\add* Bethany, the village of Mary and her sister Martha. \v 2 (Mary, whose brother Lazarus was sick, was to anoint the Lord with perfume and wipe His feet\f + \fr 11:2 \ft Literally \fqa was the one having anointed the Lord with fragrant oil and having wiped His feet\ft ; see \ref John 12:3|JHN 12:3\ref*.\f* with her hair.) \v 3 So the sisters sent word to \add Jesus\add*, “Lord, the \add one\add* You love is sick.”
\p \v 4 \add When\add* Jesus heard \add this\add*, He said, \wj “This sickness will not end in death. No, \add it is\add* for the glory of God, so that the Son of God may be glorified through it.”\wj*
\p \v 5 Now Jesus loved Martha and her sister and Lazarus. \v 6 So on hearing that \add Lazarus\add* was sick, He stayed where He was for two days, \v 7 and then He said to the disciples, \wj “Let us go back to Judea.”\wj*
\p \v 8 “Rabbi,” \add they\add* replied, “the Jews just tried to stone You, and You are going back there?”
\p \v 9 Jesus answered, \wj “Are there not twelve hours of daylight? If anyone walks in the daytime, he will not stumble, because he sees by the light of this world.\wj* \v 10 \wj But if anyone walks at night, he will stumble, because he has no light.”\wj*
\p \v 11 After He had said this, He told them, \wj “Our friend Lazarus has fallen asleep, but I am going \add there\add* to wake him up.”\wj*
\p \v 12 His disciples replied, “Lord, if he is sleeping, he will get better.” \v 13 They thought that \add Jesus\add* was talking about actual sleep, but \add He\add* was speaking about the death of \add Lazarus\add*.
\p \v 14 So Jesus told them plainly, \wj “Lazarus is dead,\wj* \v 15 \wj and for your sake I am glad I was not there, so that you may believe. But let us go to him.”\wj*
\p \v 16 Then Thomas called Didymus\f + \fr 11:16 \fqa Didymus \ft means \fqa the twin\ft .\f* said to \add his\add* fellow disciples, “\add Let\add* us also go, so that we may die with Him.”
\s1 Jesus Comforts Martha and Mary
\p \v 17 \add When\add* Jesus arrived, He found that \add Lazarus\add* had already spent four days in the tomb. \v 18 Now Bethany was near Jerusalem, a little less than two miles\f + \fr 11:18 \ft Greek \fqa about fifteen stadia\ft ; that is, approximately 1.72 miles or 2.78 kilometers\f* away, \v 19 and many of the Jews had come to Martha and Mary to console them in the loss of \add their\add* brother. \v 20 So when Martha heard that Jesus was coming, she went out to meet Him, but Mary stayed at home.
\p \v 21 Martha said to Jesus, “Lord, if You had been here, my brother would not have died. \v 22 But even now I know that God will give You whatever You ask of \add Him\add*.”
\p \v 23 \wj “Your brother will rise again,”\wj* Jesus told her.
\p \v 24 Martha replied, “I know that he will rise again in the resurrection at the last day.”
\p \v 25 Jesus said to her, \wj “I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in Me will live, even though he dies.\wj* \v 26 \wj And everyone who lives and believes in Me will never die. Do you believe this?”\wj*
\p \v 27 “Yes, Lord,” she answered, “I believe that You are the Christ, the Son of God, who was to come into the world.”
\p \v 28 \add After Martha\add* had said this, she went back and called her sister Mary aside to tell her, “The Teacher is here and is asking for you.” \v 29 And when \add Mary\add* heard this, she got up quickly and went to Him.
\p \v 30 Now Jesus \add had\add* not yet entered the village, but was still at the place where Martha had met Him. \v 31 \add When\add* the Jews who were in the house consoling \add Mary\add* saw how quickly \add she\add* got up and went out, they followed her, supposing she was going to the tomb to mourn there. \v 32 When Mary came to Jesus \add and\add* saw Him, she fell at His feet \add and\add* said, “Lord, if You had been here, my brother \add would\add* not have died.”
\p \v 33 When Jesus saw her weeping, and the Jews who had come with her \add also\add* weeping, He was deeply moved in spirit\f + \fr 11:33 \ft Or \fqa He was indignant in spirit\ft ; similarly in verse 38\f* and troubled. \v 34 \wj “Where have you put him?”\wj* He asked.
\p “Come and see, Lord,” they answered.
\p \v 35 Jesus wept.
\p \v 36 Then the Jews said, “See how He loved him!”
\p \v 37 But some of them asked, “Could not this \add man\add* who opened the eyes of the blind also have kept \add Lazarus\add* from dying?”
\s1 Jesus Raises Lazarus
\r (\ref Acts 9:36–43|ACT 9:36-43\ref*)
\p \v 38 Jesus, once again deeply moved, came to the tomb. It was a cave \add with\add* a stone laid across \add the entrance\add*. \v 39 \wj “Take away the stone,”\wj* Jesus said.
\p “Lord, by now he stinks,” said Martha, the sister of the dead \add man\add*. “It has already been four days.”
\p \v 40 Jesus replied, \wj “Did I not tell you that if you believed, you would see the glory of God?”\wj*
\p \v 41 So they took away the stone. Then Jesus lifted His eyes upward and said, \wj “Father, I thank You that You have heard Me.\wj* \v 42 \wj I knew that You always hear Me, but I say \add this\add* for the benefit of the people standing \add here\add*, so they may believe that You sent Me.”\wj*
\p \v 43 \add After Jesus\add* had said this, He called out in a loud voice, \wj “Lazarus, come out!”\wj*
\p \v 44 The \add man who\add* had been dead came out \add with his\add* hands and feet bound in strips of linen, and his face wrapped in a cloth.\f + \fr 11:44 \ft Greek \fqa soudariō\f*
\p \wj “Unwrap him and let him go,”\wj* Jesus told them.
\s1 The Plot to Kill Jesus
\r (\ref Matthew 26:1–5|MAT 26:1-5\ref*; \ref Mark 14:1–2|MRK 14:1-2\ref*; \ref Luke 22:1–2|LUK 22:1-2\ref*)
\p \v 45 Therefore many of the Jews who had come to Mary, and had seen what \add Jesus\add* did, believed in Him. \v 46 But some of them went to the Pharisees and told them what Jesus had done.
\p \v 47 Then the chief priests and Pharisees convened the Sanhedrin\f + \fr 11:47 \ft Or \fqa the Council\f* and said, “What are we to do? This man is performing many signs. \v 48 If we let Him go on like this, everyone will believe in Him, and then the Romans will come and take away both our place and \add our\add* nation.”
\p \v 49 But one of them, \add named\add* Caiaphas, who was high priest that year, said to them, “You know nothing at all! \v 50 You do not realize that it is better for you that one man die for the people \add than that\add* the whole nation perish.”
\p \v 51 \add Caiaphas\add* did not say this on his own. Instead, \add as\add* high priest that year, he was prophesying that Jesus would die for the nation, \v 52 and not only for the nation, but also \add for\add* the scattered children of God, to gather \add them\add* together into one.
\p \v 53 So from that day \add on\add* they plotted to kill Him. \v 54 As a result, Jesus no longer went about publicly among the Jews, but He withdrew to a town called Ephraim in an area near the wilderness. \add And\add* He stayed there with the disciples.
\p \v 55 Now the Jewish Passover was near, and many \add people\add* went up from the country to Jerusalem to purify themselves before the Passover. \v 56 They kept looking for Jesus and asking one another \add as\add* they stood in the temple \add courts\add*,\f + \fr 11:56 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\f* “What do you think? Will He come to the feast at all?” \v 57 But the chief priests and Pharisees had given orders that anyone \add who\add* knew where He was must report \add it\add*, so that they could arrest Him.
\c 12
\s1 Mary Anoints Jesus
\r (\ref Matthew 26:6–13|MAT 26:6-13\ref*; \ref Mark 14:3–9|MRK 14:3-9\ref*; \ref Luke 7:36–50|LUK 7:36-50\ref*)
\p \v 1 Six days before the Passover, Jesus came to Bethany, \add the hometown of\add* Lazarus, whom \add He\add* had raised from \add the\add* dead. \v 2 So they hosted a dinner for \add Jesus\add* there. Martha served, and Lazarus was among those reclining at the table with Him. \v 3 Then Mary took about a pint\f + \fr 12:3 \ft Greek \fqa a litra\ft ; that is, approximately 12 ounces or 340 grams\f* of expensive perfume, made of pure nard, \add and\add* she anointed Jesus’ feet and wiped \add them\add* with her hair. And the house was filled with the fragrance of the perfume.
\p \v 4 But one of His disciples, Judas Iscariot, who was going to betray Him, asked, \v 5 “Why wasn’t this perfume sold for three hundred denarii\f + \fr 12:5 \ft A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see \ref Matthew 20:2|MAT 20:2\ref*.\f* and \add the money\add* given to \add the\add* poor?” \v 6 \add Judas\add* did not say this because he cared about the poor, but because he was a thief. As keeper of the money bag, he used to take from what was put into \add it\add*.
\p \v 7 \wj “Leave her alone,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “She has kept \add this perfume\add* in preparation for the day of My burial.\wj* \v 8 \wj The poor you will always have with you,\f + \fr 12:8 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 15:11|DEU 15:11\ref*.\f* but \add you will\add* not always have Me.”\wj*
\s1 The Plot to Kill Lazarus
\p \v 9 Meanwhile a large crowd of Jews learned that \add Jesus\add* was there. And they came not only because of \add Him\add*, but also to see Lazarus, whom He had raised from \add the\add* dead. \v 10 \add So\add* the chief priests made plans to kill Lazarus as well, \v 11 for on account of him many of the Jews were deserting \add them\add* and believing in Jesus.
\s1 The Triumphal Entry
\r (\ref Zechariah 9:9–13|ZEC 9:9-13\ref*; \ref Matthew 21:1–11|MAT 21:1-11\ref*; \ref Mark 11:1–11|MRK 11:1-11\ref*; \ref Luke 19:28–40|LUK 19:28-40\ref*)
\p \v 12 The next day the great crowd that had come to the feast heard that Jesus was coming to Jerusalem. \v 13 They took palm branches and went out to meet Him, shouting:
\b 
\q1 “Hosanna!”\f + \fr 12:13 \fqa Hosanna \ft is a transliteration of the Hebrew \fqa Hosia-na\ft , meaning \fqa Save, we pray \ft or \fqa Save now\ft , which became a shout of praise; see \ref Psalm 118:25|PSA 118:25\ref*.\f*
\b 
\q1 “Blessed is He who comes in \add the\add* name of \add the\add* Lord!”\f + \fr 12:13 \ref Psalm 118:26|PSA 118:26\ref*\f*
\b 
\q1 “ \add Blessed is \add* the King of Israel!”
\p \v 14 Finding a young donkey, Jesus sat on it, as it is written:
\b 
\q1 \v 15 “Do not be afraid, O Daughter of Zion.
\q2 See, your King is coming,
\q2 seated on the colt of a donkey.”\f + \fr 12:15 \ref Zechariah 9:9|ZEC 9:9\ref*\f*
\p \v 16 At first His disciples \add did\add* not understand these things, but after Jesus was glorified they remembered what had been done to Him, \add and they realized\add* that these very things had also \add been\add* written about Him.
\p \v 17 Meanwhile, many people who had been with \add Jesus\add* when He called Lazarus from the tomb and raised him from \add the\add* dead continued to testify. \v 18 That is also why the crowd went out to meet Him, because they heard that He had performed this sign.
\p \v 19 Then the Pharisees said to one another, “You can see that this is doing you no good. Look \add how\add* the whole world has gone after Him!”
\s1 Jesus Predicts His Death
\p \v 20 Now there were some Greeks among those who went up to worship at the feast. \v 21 They came to Philip, who was from Bethsaida in Galilee, and requested of him, “Sir, we want to see Jesus.” \v 22 Philip relayed \add this appeal\add* to Andrew, \add and both of them\add* went and told Jesus.
\p \v 23 But Jesus replied, \wj “The hour has come for the Son of Man to be glorified.\wj* \v 24 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, unless a kernel of wheat falls to the ground \add and\add* dies, it remains only \add a seed\add*. But if it dies, it bears much fruit.\wj* \v 25 \wj Whoever loves his life will lose it, \add but\add* whoever hates his life in this world will keep it for eternal life.\wj* \v 26 \wj If anyone serves Me, he must follow Me; and where I am, My servant will be as well. If anyone serves Me, the Father will honor him.\wj*
\p \v 27 \wj Now My soul is troubled, and what shall I say? ‘Father, save Me from this hour’? No, it is for this purpose that I have come to this hour.\wj* \v 28 \wj Father, glorify Your name!”\wj*
\p Then a voice came from heaven: “I have glorified \add it\add*, and I will glorify \add it\add* again.”
\p \v 29 The crowd standing \add there\add* heard \add it and\add* said that it had thundered. Others said that an angel had spoken to Him.
\p \v 30 In response, Jesus said, \wj “This voice was not for My benefit, but yours.\wj* \v 31 \wj Now judgment is upon this world; now the prince of this world will be cast out.\wj* \v 32 \wj And I, when I am lifted up from the earth, will draw everyone to Myself.”\wj* \v 33 He said this to indicate the kind of death He was going to die.
\p \v 34 The crowd replied, “We have heard from the Law that the Christ will remain forever. \add So\add* how \add can\add* You say that the Son of Man must be lifted up? Who is this Son of Man?”
\p \v 35 Then Jesus told them, \wj “\add For\add* a little while longer, the Light will be among you. Walk while you have the Light, so that darkness will not overtake you. The \add one who\add* walks in the darkness \add does\add* not know where he is going.\wj* \v 36 \wj While you have the Light, believe in the Light, so that you may become sons of light.”\wj*
\p \add After\add* Jesus had spoken these things, He went away \add and\add* was hidden from them.
\s1 Belief and Unbelief
\p \v 37 Although \add Jesus\add* had performed so many signs in their presence, they still did not believe in Him. \v 38 This was to fulfill the word of Isaiah the prophet:
\b 
\q1 “Lord, who has believed our message?
\q2 And to whom \add has\add* the arm of \add the\add* Lord been revealed?”\f + \fr 12:38 \ref Isaiah 53:1|ISA 53:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 39 For this reason they were unable to believe. For again, Isaiah says:
\b 
\q1 \v 40 “He has blinded their eyes
\q2 and hardened their hearts,
\q1 so that they cannot see with \add their\add* eyes,
\q2 and understand with \add their\add* hearts,
\q1 and turn,
\q2 and I would heal them.”\f + \fr 12:40 \ref Isaiah 6:10|ISA 6:10\ref*\f*
\p \v 41 Isaiah said these things because he saw \add Jesus’\add* glory and spoke about Him. \v 42 Nevertheless, many of the leaders believed in Him. But because of the Pharisees they did not confess \add Him\add*, for fear that they would be put out of the synagogue. \v 43 For they loved praise from men more than praise from God.
\p \v 44 Then Jesus cried out, \wj “Whoever believes in Me \add does\add* not believe in Me \add alone\add*, but in the \add One who\add* sent Me.\wj* \v 45 \wj And whoever sees Me sees the \add One who\add* sent Me.\wj* \v 46 \wj I have come into the world \add as\add* a light, so that no one who believes in Me should remain in darkness.\wj*
\p \v 47 \wj As for anyone who hears My words and \add does\add* not keep \add them\add*, I \add do\add* not judge him. For I have not come to judge the world, but to save the world.\wj* \v 48 \wj There is a judge \add for\add* the \add one who\add* rejects Me and does not receive My words: The word that I have spoken will judge him on the last day.\wj*
\p \v 49 \wj I \add have\add* not spoken on My own, but the Father \add who\add* sent Me has commanded Me what to say and how to say it.\wj* \v 50 \wj And I know that His command leads to eternal life. So I speak exactly what the Father has told Me to say.”\wj*
\c 13
\s1 Jesus Washes His Disciples’ Feet
\p \v 1 \add It was\add* now just before the Passover Feast, \add and\add* Jesus knew that His hour had come to leave this world \add and return\add* to the Father. Having loved \add His\add* own who \add were\add* in the world, He loved them to \add the\add* very end.\f + \fr 13:1 \ft Or \fqa He showed them the full extent of His love.\f* \v 2 The evening meal was underway, \add and\add* the devil \add had\add* already put into the heart of Judas, \add the son\add* of Simon Iscariot, to betray \add Jesus\add*.
\p \v 3 \add Jesus\add* knew that the Father had delivered all things into \add His\add* hands, and that He had come from God and was returning to God. \v 4 \add So\add* He got up from the supper, laid aside \add His\add* outer garments, and wrapped a towel \add around\add* His \add waist\add*. \v 5 After that, He poured water into a basin and began to wash the disciples’ feet and dry \add them\add* with the towel that was around Him.
\p \v 6 He came to Simon Peter, who asked Him, “Lord, are You going to wash my feet?”
\p \v 7 Jesus replied, \wj “You \add do\add* not realize now what I am doing, but later you will understand.”\wj*
\p \v 8 “Never shall You wash my feet!” Peter told Him.
\p Jesus answered, \wj “Unless I wash you, you have no part with Me.”\wj*
\p \v 9 “\add Then,\add* Lord,” Simon Peter replied, “not only my feet, but \add my\add* hands and \add my\add* head as well!”
\p \v 10 Jesus told him, \wj “Whoever has already bathed needs only to wash \add his\add* feet, \add and\add* he will be completely clean. And you are clean, though not all \add of you\add*.”\wj* \v 11 For He knew who would betray Him. That is why He said, \wj “Not all of you are clean.”\wj*
\p \v 12 When \add Jesus\add* had washed their feet and put on His outer garments, He reclined with them again \add and\add* asked, \wj “Do you know what I have done for you?\wj* \v 13 \wj You call Me Teacher and Lord, and rightly \add so\add*, because I am.\wj* \v 14 \wj So if I, \add your\add* Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also should wash one another’s feet.\wj* \v 15 \wj I have set you an example so that you should do as I have done for you.\wj* \v 16 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, no servant is greater than his master,\f + \fr 13:16 \ft Cited in \ref John 15:20|JHN 15:20\ref*\f* nor \add is\add* a messenger greater than the \add one who\add* sent him.\wj* \v 17 \wj If you know these things, you will be blessed if you do them.\wj*
\s1 Jesus Predicts His Betrayal
\r (\ref Psalm 41:1–13|PSA 41:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 18 \wj I am not speaking about all of you; I know whom I have chosen. But this is to fulfill the Scripture: ‘The one who shares My bread has lifted up his heel against Me.’\f + \fr 13:18 \ref Psalm 41:9|PSA 41:9\ref*\f*\wj* \v 19 \wj I am telling you now before it happens, so that when it comes to pass, you will believe that I am \add He\add*.\wj* \v 20 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, whoever receives the one I send receives Me, and whoever receives Me receives the \add One who\add* sent Me.”\wj*
\p \v 21 \add After\add* Jesus had said \add this\add*, He became troubled in spirit and testified, \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, one of you will betray Me.”\wj*
\p \v 22 The disciples looked at one another, perplexed as to which of them He meant. \v 23 One of His disciples, the one whom Jesus loved, was reclining at \add His\add* side.\f + \fr 13:23 \ft Greek \fqa was reclining in the bosom of Jesus\f* \v 24 So Simon Peter motioned to him to ask \add Jesus\add* which one He was talking about. \v 25 Leaning back against Jesus, he asked, “Lord, who is it?”
\p \v 26 Jesus answered, \wj “It is the \add one\add* to whom I give \add this\add* morsel after I have dipped \add it\add*.”\wj* Then He dipped the morsel and gave \add it\add* to Judas \add son\add* of Simon Iscariot. \v 27 \add And when Judas had taken\add* the morsel, Satan entered into him.
\p Then Jesus said to \add Judas\add*, \wj “What you are about to do, do quickly.”\wj* \v 28 But no one \add at the table\add* knew why \add Jesus\add* had said this to him. \v 29 Since Judas kept the money bag, some thought that Jesus was telling him to buy what was needed for the feast, or to give something to the poor. \v 30 As soon as he had received the morsel, \add Judas\add* went out \add into the\add* night.
\s1 Love One Another
\r (\ref Romans 12:9–13|ROM 12:9-13\ref*; \ref 1 John 3:11–24|1JN 3:11-24\ref*)
\p \v 31 When \add Judas\add* had gone out, Jesus said, \wj “Now the Son of Man is glorified, and God is glorified in Him.\wj* \v 32 \wj If God is glorified in Him,\f + \fr 13:32 \ft WH does not include \fqa If God is glorified in Him\ft .\f* God will also glorify \add the Son\add* in Himself — and will glorify Him at once.\wj*
\p \v 33 \wj Little children, I am with you only a little while longer. You will look for Me, and as I said to the Jews, so now I say to you: ‘Where I am going, you cannot come.’\wj*
\p \v 34 \wj A new commandment I give you: Love one another. As I have loved you, so you also must love one another.\wj* \v 35 \wj By this everyone will know that you are My disciples, if you love one another.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Predicts Peter’s Denial
\r (\ref Matthew 26:31–35|MAT 26:31-35\ref*; \ref Mark 14:27–31|MRK 14:27-31\ref*; \ref Luke 22:31–38|LUK 22:31-38\ref*)
\p \v 36 “Lord, where are You going?” Simon Peter asked.
\p Jesus answered, \wj “Where I am going, you cannot follow Me now, but you will follow later.”\wj*
\p \v 37 “Lord,” said Peter, “why can’t I follow You now? I will lay down my life for You.”
\p \v 38 \wj “Will you lay down your life for Me?”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “Truly, truly, I tell you, \add before the\add* rooster crows, you will deny Me three times.’’\wj*
\c 14
\s1 In My Father’s House Are Many Rooms
\p \v 1 \wj \add “Do\add* not \add let\add* your hearts be troubled. You believe in God;\f + \fr 14:1 \ft Or \fqa Believe in God\f* believe in Me as well.\wj* \v 2 \wj In My Father’s house are many rooms. If \add it were\add* not so, would I have told you that I am going \add there\add* to prepare a place for you?\f + \fr 14:2 \ft Or \fqa If it were not so, I would have told you. I am going there to prepare a place for you.\f*\wj* \v 3 \wj And if I go and prepare a place for you, I will come back and welcome you into My presence, so that you also may be where I am.\wj* \v 4 \wj You know the way to the place where I am going.\f + \fr 14:4 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa And where I go you know, and the way you know.\f*”\wj*
\s1 The Way, the Truth, and the Life
\p \v 5 “Lord,” said Thomas, “we do not know where You are going, \add so\add* how can we know the way?”
\p \v 6 Jesus answered, \wj “I am the way and the truth and the life. No one comes to the Father except through Me.\wj* \v 7 \wj If you had known Me, you would know My Father as well. From now on you do know Him and have seen Him.”\wj*
\p \v 8 Philip said to Him, “Lord, show us the Father, and that will be enough for us.”
\p \v 9 Jesus replied, \wj “Philip, I have been with you all this time, and still you do not know Me? Anyone who has seen Me has seen the Father. How \add can\add* you say, ‘Show us the Father’?\wj* \v 10 \wj Do you not believe that I \add am\add* in the Father and the Father is in Me? The words I say to you, I do not speak on My own. Instead, \add it is\add* the Father dwelling in Me, performing His works.\wj* \v 11 \wj Believe Me that I \add am\add* in the Father and the Father \add is\add* in Me — or at least believe on account of the works themselves.\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, whoever believes in Me will also do the works that I am doing. He will do even greater \add things\add* than these, because I am going to the Father.\wj* \v 13 \wj And I will do whatever you ask in My name, so that the Father may be glorified in the Son.\wj* \v 14 \wj If you ask Me\f + \fr 14:14 \ft TR does not include \fqa Me\ft .\f* for anything in My name, I will do \add it\add*.\wj*
\s1 Jesus Promises the Holy Spirit
\r (\ref John 16:5–16|JHN 16:5-16\ref*)
\p \v 15 \wj If you love Me, you will keep\f + \fr 14:15 \ft Or \fqa If you love Me, keep\f* My commandments.\wj* \v 16 \wj And I will ask the Father, and He will give you another Advocate\f + \fr 14:16 \ft Or \fqa Comforter \ft or \fqa Helper \ft or \fqa Counselor\ft ; Greek \fqa Paraclete\ft ; also in verse 26\f* to be with you forever —\wj* \v 17 \wj the Spirit of truth. The world cannot receive \add Him\add*, because it neither sees Him nor knows \add Him\add*. But you do know Him, for He abides with you and will be in you.\f + \fr 14:17 \ft WH \fqa and is in you\f*\wj*
\p \v 18 \wj I will not leave you as orphans; I will come to you.\wj* \v 19 \wj In a little while the world will see Me no more, but you will see Me. Because I live, you also will live.\wj* \v 20 \wj On that day you will know that I \add am\add* in My Father, and you \add are\add* in Me, and I \add am\add* in you.\wj* \v 21 \wj Whoever has My commandments and keeps them is the \add one who\add* loves Me. The \add one who\add* loves Me will be loved by My Father, and I will love him and reveal Myself to him.”\wj*
\p \v 22 Judas (not Iscariot) asked Him, “Lord, why are You going to reveal Yourself to us and not to the world?”
\p \v 23 Jesus replied, \wj “If anyone loves Me, he will keep My word. My Father will love him, and We will come to him and make Our home with him.\wj* \v 24 \wj Whoever does not love Me does not keep My words. The word that you hear is not My own, but \add it is \add* from the Father who sent Me.\wj*
\p \v 25 \wj All this I have spoken to you \add while\add* I am still with you.\wj* \v 26 \wj But the Advocate, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in My name, will teach you all things and will remind you of everything I have told you.\wj*
\s1 Peace I Leave with You
\p \v 27 \wj Peace I leave with you; My peace I give to you. I \add do\add* not give to you as the world gives. \add Do\add* not \add let\add* your hearts be troubled; \add do\add* not be afraid.\wj* \v 28 \wj You heard Me say, ‘I am going away, and I am coming \add back\add* to you.’ If you loved Me, you would rejoice that I am going to the Father, because the Father is greater than I.\wj* \v 29 \wj And now I have told you before it happens, so that when it does happen, you will believe.\wj*
\p \v 30 \wj I will \add not\add* speak with you much \add longer\add*, for the prince of this world is coming, and he has no claim on Me.\wj* \v 31 \wj But I do exactly what the Father has commanded Me, so that the world may know that I love the Father.\wj*
\p \wj Get up! Let us go on from here.’’\wj*
\c 15
\s1 Jesus the True Vine
\r (\ref Isaiah 27:1–13|ISA 27:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 1 \wj \add “\add*I am the true vine, and My Father is the keeper of the vineyard.\wj* \v 2 \wj He cuts off every branch in Me that bears no fruit, and every \add branch\add* that does bear fruit, He prunes to make it even more fruitful.\wj* \v 3 \wj You are already clean because of the word I have spoken to you.\wj* \v 4 \wj Remain in Me, and I \add will remain\add* in you. Just as no branch can bear fruit by itself unless it remains in the vine, neither \add can\add* you \add bear fruit\add* unless you remain in Me.\wj*
\p \v 5 \wj I am the vine \add and\add* you \add are\add* the branches. The \add one who\add* remains in Me, and I in him, will bear much fruit. For apart from Me you can do nothing.\wj* \v 6 \wj If anyone \add does\add* not remain in Me, \add he is\add* like a branch that is thrown away and withers. \add Such branches\add* are gathered up, thrown into the fire, and burned.\wj* \v 7 \wj If you remain in Me and My words remain in you, ask whatever you wish, and it will be done for you.\wj* \v 8 \wj This \add is to\add* My Father’s glory, that you bear much fruit, \add proving\add* yourselves to be My disciples.\wj*
\s1 No Greater Love
\p \v 9 \wj As the Father has loved Me, so have I loved you. Remain in My love.\wj* \v 10 \wj If you keep My commandments, you will remain in My love, just as I have kept My Father’s commandments and remain in His love.\wj* \v 11 \wj I have told you these things so that My joy may be in you and your joy may be complete.\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj This is My commandment, that you love one another as I have loved you.\wj* \v 13 \wj Greater love has no one than this, that \add he\add* lay down his life for his friends.\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj You are My friends if you do what I command you.\wj* \v 15 \wj No longer do I call you servants, for a servant \add does\add* not understand what his master is doing. But I have called you friends, because everything I have learned from My Father I have made known to you.\wj* \v 16 \wj You \add did\add* not choose Me, but I chose you. And I appointed you to go and bear fruit — fruit that will remain — so that whatever you ask the Father in My name, He will give you.\wj* \v 17 \wj This is My command to you: Love one another.\wj*
\s1 The Hatred of the World
\p \v 18 \wj If the world hates you, understand that it hated Me \add first\add*.\wj* \v 19 \wj If you were of the world, \add it\add* would love \add you as\add* its own. Instead, the world hates you, because you are not of the world, but I have chosen you out of the world.\wj*
\p \v 20 \wj Remember the word that I spoke to you: ‘No servant is greater than his master.’\f + \fr 15:20 \ref John 13:16|JHN 13:16\ref*\f* If they persecuted Me, they will persecute you as well; if they kept My word, they will keep yours as well.\wj* \v 21 \wj But they will treat you like this because of My name, since they do not know the \add One who\add* sent Me.\wj* \v 22 \wj If I had not come and spoken to them, they would not be guilty of sin. Now, however, they have no excuse for their sin.\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj Whoever hates Me hates My Father as well.\wj* \v 24 \wj If I had not done among them the works that no one else did, they would not be guilty of sin; but now they have seen and hated both Me and My Father.\wj* \v 25 \wj But \add this is\add* to fulfill \add what\add* is written in their Law: ‘They hated Me without reason.’\f + \fr 15:25 \ft See \ref Psalm 35:19|PSA 35:19\ref*, Psalm 38:19, and Psalm 69:4.\f*\wj*
\p \v 26 \wj When the Advocate\f + \fr 15:26 \ft Or \fqa Comforter \ft or \fqa Helper \ft or \fqa Counselor\ft ; Greek \fqa Paraclete\f* comes, whom I will send to you from the Father — the Spirit of truth who proceeds from the Father — He will testify about Me.\wj* \v 27 \wj And you also must testify, because you have been with Me from \add the\add* beginning.’’\wj*
\c 16
\s1 Persecution Foretold
\r (\ref Acts 23:12–22|ACT 23:12-22\ref*)
\p \v 1 \wj \add “\add*I have told you these things so that you will not fall away.\wj* \v 2 \wj They will put you out of the synagogues. In fact, a time is coming when anyone who kills you will think he is offering a service to God.\wj* \v 3 \wj They will do these things because they have not known the Father \add or\add* Me.\wj* \v 4 \wj But I have told you these things so that when their hour comes, you will remember that I told you \add about\add* them. I did not tell you these things from \add the\add* beginning, because I was with you.\wj*
\s1 The Promise of the Holy Spirit
\r (\ref John 14:15–26|JHN 14:15-26\ref*)
\p \v 5 \wj Now, however, I am going to Him who sent Me; yet none of you asks Me, ‘Where are You going?’\wj* \v 6 \wj Instead, your hearts are filled with sorrow because I have told you these things.\wj* \v 7 \wj But I tell you the truth, it is for your benefit that I am going away. Unless I go away, the Advocate\f + \fr 16:7 \ft Or \fqa Comforter \ft or \fqa Helper \ft or \fqa Counselor\ft ; Greek \fqa Paraclete\f* will not come to you; but if I go, I will send Him to you.\wj*
\p \v 8 \wj And \add when\add* He comes, He will convict the world in regard to sin and righteousness and judgment:\wj* \v 9 \wj in regard to sin, because they do not believe in Me;\wj* \v 10 \wj in regard to righteousness, because I am going to the Father and you will no longer see Me;\wj* \v 11 \wj and in regard to judgment, because the prince of this world has been condemned.\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj I still have much to tell you, but you cannot yet bear \add to hear it\add*.\wj* \v 13 \wj However, when the Spirit of truth comes, He will guide you into all truth. For He will not speak on His own, but He will speak what He hears, and He will declare to you what is to come.\wj* \v 14 \wj He will glorify Me by taking from what \add is\add* Mine and disclosing \add it\add* to you.\wj* \v 15 \wj Everything that belongs to the Father is Mine. That is why I said that \add the Spirit\add* will take from what \add is\add* Mine and disclose \add it\add* to you.\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj In a little \add while\add* you will see Me no more, and then after a little \add while\add* you will see Me.\f + \fr 16:16 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa because I go away to the Father.\f*”\wj*
\s1 Grief Will Turn to Joy
\p \v 17 Then \add some\add* of His disciples asked one another, “Why is He telling us, \wj ‘In a little \add while\add* you will not see Me, and then after a little \add while\add* you will see Me’\wj* and \wj ‘Because I am going to the Father’\wj*?” \v 18 They kept asking, “Why is He saying, \wj ‘a little \add while\add*’\wj*? We do not understand what He is saying.”
\p \v 19 Aware that they wanted to question Him, \add Jesus\add* said to them, \wj “Are you asking one another why I said, ‘In a little \add while\add* you will not see Me, and then after a little \add while\add* you will see Me’?\wj* \v 20 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, you will weep and wail \add while\add* the world rejoices. You will grieve, but your grief will turn to joy.\wj* \v 21 \wj A woman has pain in childbirth because her time has come; but when she brings forth \add her\add* child, she forgets \add her\add* anguish because of \add her\add* joy that a child has been born into the world.\wj* \v 22 \wj So also you have sorrow now, but I will see you again and your hearts will rejoice, and no one will take away your joy.\wj*
\s1 Ask in My Name
\r (\ref Matthew 18:19–20|MAT 18:19-20\ref*)
\p \v 23 \wj In that day you will \add no longer\add* ask Me \add anything\add*. Truly, truly, I tell you, whatever you ask the Father in My name, He will give you.\wj* \v 24 \wj Until now you have not asked \add for anything\add* in My name. Ask and you will receive, so that your joy may be complete.\wj*
\p \v 25 \wj I have spoken these things to you in figures of speech. An hour is coming when I will no longer speak to you \add this way\add*, but will tell you plainly about the Father.\wj* \v 26 \wj In that day you will ask in My name. I am not saying that I will ask the Father on your behalf.\wj* \v 27 \wj For the Father Himself loves you, because you have loved Me and have believed that I came from God.\f + \fr 16:27 \ft WH \fqa from the Father\f*\wj* \v 28 \wj I came from the Father and entered the world. In turn, I will leave the world and go to the Father.”\wj*
\p \v 29 His disciples said, “See, now You are speaking plainly and without figures of speech. \v 30 Now we understand that You know all things and that You have no need for anyone to question You. Because of this, we believe that You came from God.”
\p \v 31 \wj “Do you finally believe?”\wj* Jesus replied. \v 32 \wj “Look, an hour is coming and has already come when you will be scattered, each to \add his\add* own \add home\add*, \add and\add* you will leave Me all alone. Yet I am not alone, because the Father is with Me.\wj* \v 33 \wj I have told you these things so that in Me you may have peace. In the world you will have tribulation. But take courage; I have overcome the world!”\wj*
\c 17
\s1 Prayer for the Son
\p \v 1 \add When\add* Jesus had spoken these things, He lifted up His eyes to heaven \add and\add* said, \wj “Father, the hour has come. Glorify Your Son, that \add Your\add* Son may glorify You.\wj* \v 2 \wj For You granted Him authority over all people,\f + \fr 17:2 \ft Literally \fqa all flesh\f* so that He may give eternal life \add to\add* all those You have given Him.\wj* \v 3 \wj Now this is eternal life, that they may know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom You have sent.\wj* \v 4 \wj I have glorified You on earth by accomplishing the work You gave Me to do.\wj* \v 5 \wj And now, Father, glorify Me in Your presence with the glory I had with You before the world existed.\wj*
\s1 Prayer for the Disciples
\p \v 6 \wj I have revealed Your name to those You have given Me out of the world. They were Yours; You gave them to Me, and they have kept Your word.\wj* \v 7 \wj Now they know that everything You have given Me comes from You.\wj* \v 8 \wj For I have given them the words You gave Me, and they have received \add them\add*. They knew with certainty that I came from You, and they believed that You sent Me.\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj I ask on their behalf. I do not ask on behalf of the world, but on behalf of those You have given Me; for they are Yours.\wj* \v 10 \wj All I have is Yours, and \add all\add* You have \add is\add* Mine; and in them I have been glorified.\wj* \v 11 \wj I will no longer be in the world, but they are in the world, and I am coming to You.\wj*
\p \wj Holy Father, protect them by Your name, \add the name\add* You gave Me,\f + \fr 17:11 \ft Literally \fqa Your name, which You gave Me\ft ; TR \fqa Your name. These You have given Me\ft ; similarly in verse 12\f* so that they may be one as We \add are one\add*.\wj* \v 12 \wj While I was with them, I protected and preserved them by Your name, \add the name\add* You gave Me. Not one of them has been lost, except the son of destruction, so that the Scripture would be fulfilled.\wj*
\p \v 13 \wj But now I am coming to You; and I am saying these things \add while I am\add* in the world, so that they may have My joy fulfilled within them.\wj* \v 14 \wj I have given them Your word and the world has hated them. For they are not of the world, just as I am not of the world.\wj*
\p \v 15 \wj I am not asking that You take them out of the world, but that You keep them from the evil \add one\add*.\f + \fr 17:15 \ft Or \fqa from evil\f*\wj* \v 16 \wj They are not of the world, just as I am not of the world.\wj* \v 17 \wj Sanctify them by the truth; Your word is truth.\wj* \v 18 \wj As You sent Me into the world, I \add have\add* also sent them into the world.\wj* \v 19 \wj For them I sanctify Myself, so that they too may be sanctified by \add the\add* truth.\wj*
\s1 Prayer for All Believers
\p \v 20 \wj I am not asking on behalf of them alone, but also on behalf of those who will believe in Me through their message,\wj* \v 21 \wj that all of them may be one, as You, Father, \add are\add* in Me, and I \add am\add* in You. \add May\add* they also be in Us, so that the world may believe that You sent Me.\wj*
\p \v 22 \wj I have given them the glory You gave Me, so that they may be one as We \add are\add* one —\wj* \v 23 \wj I in them and You in Me — that they may be perfectly united, so that the world may know that You sent Me and have loved them just as You have loved Me.\wj*
\p \v 24 \wj Father, I want \add those\add* You have given Me to be with Me where I am, that they may see \add the\add* glory You gave Me because You loved Me before \add the\add* foundation of \add the\add* world.\wj*
\p \v 25 \wj Righteous Father, although the world \add has\add* not known You, I know You, and \add they\add* know that You sent Me.\wj* \v 26 \wj And I have made Your name \add known\add* to them and will continue to make \add it\add* known, so that the love You have for Me may be in them, and I in them.”\wj*
\c 18
\s1 The Betrayal of Jesus
\r (\ref Matthew 26:47–56|MAT 26:47-56\ref*; \ref Mark 14:43–52|MRK 14:43-52\ref*; \ref Luke 22:47–53|LUK 22:47-53\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add After\add* Jesus had spoken these \add words\add*, \add He\add* went out with His disciples across the Kidron Valley, where \add they\add* entered a garden. \v 2 Now Judas His betrayer also knew the place, because Jesus\f + \fr 18:2 \ft HF and PT \fqa Jesus also\f* \add had\add* often met there with His disciples. \v 3 So Judas brought a band of soldiers and officers from the chief priests and Pharisees. They arrived \add at the garden carrying\add* lanterns, torches, and weapons.
\p \v 4 Jesus, knowing all that was coming upon Him, stepped forward and asked them, \wj “Whom are you seeking?”\wj*
\p \v 5 “Jesus of Nazareth,” they answered.
\p \add Jesus\add* said, \wj “I am \add He\add*.”\wj*
\p And Judas His betrayer was standing \add there\add* with them. \v 6 When \add Jesus\add* said, \wj “I am \add He\add*,”\wj* they drew back and fell to \add the\add* ground.
\p \v 7 So He asked them again, \wj “Whom are you seeking?”\wj*
\p “Jesus of Nazareth,” they answered.
\p \v 8 \wj “I told you that I am \add He\add*,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “So if you are looking for Me, let these \add men\add* go.”\wj* \v 9 This was to fulfill the word He had spoken: \wj “I have not lost one of those You have given Me.”\wj*\f + \fr 18:9 \ft See \ref John 6:39|JHN 6:39\ref* and John 17:12.\f*
\p \v 10 Then Simon Peter drew \add his\add* sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his right ear. The servant’s name was Malchus.
\p \v 11 \wj “Put \add your\add* sword back in \add its\add* sheath!”\wj* Jesus said to Peter. \wj “Shall I not drink the cup the Father has given Me?”\wj*
\p \v 12 Then the band of soldiers, \add with its\add* commander and the officers of the Jews, arrested Jesus and bound Him. \v 13 They brought \add Him\add* first to Annas, who was \add the\add* father-in-law of Caiaphas, \add the\add* high priest \add that\add* year. \v 14 Caiaphas was the \add one\add* who had advised the Jews that it would be better if one man died for the people.
\s1 Peter’s First Denial
\r (\ref Matthew 26:69–70|MAT 26:69-70\ref*; \ref Mark 14:66–68|MRK 14:66-68\ref*; \ref Luke 22:54–57|LUK 22:54-57\ref*)
\p \v 15 Now Simon Peter and another disciple were following Jesus. Since that disciple was known to the high priest, he also went with Jesus into the courtyard of the high priest. \v 16 But Peter stood outside at the door. Then the disciple who was known to the high priest went out and spoke to the doorkeeper, and brought Peter \add in\add*.
\p \v 17 At this, the servant girl watching the door said to Peter, “Aren’t you also \add one\add* of this man’s disciples?”
\p “I am not,” he answered.
\p \v 18 Because it was cold, the servants and officers were standing around a charcoal fire they had made to keep warm. And Peter was also standing with them, warming himself.
\s1 Jesus before the High Priest
\r (\ref Matthew 26:57–68|MAT 26:57-68\ref*; \ref Mark 14:53–65|MRK 14:53-65\ref*; \ref Luke 22:66–71|LUK 22:66-71\ref*)
\p \v 19 Meanwhile, the high priest questioned Jesus about His disciples and His teaching.
\p \v 20 \wj “I have spoken openly to the world,”\wj* Jesus answered. \wj “I always taught in \add the\add* synagogues and at the temple, where all the Jews come together. I said nothing in secret.\wj* \v 21 \wj Why are you asking Me? Ask those who heard My message. Surely they know what I said.”\wj*
\p \v 22 \add When Jesus\add* had said \add this\add*, one of the officers standing nearby slapped \add Him\add* in the face \add and\add* said, “\add Is\add* this how You answer the high priest?”
\p \v 23 Jesus replied, \wj “If I said \add something\add* wrong, testify as to what was wrong. But if \add I spoke\add* correctly, why did you strike Me?”\wj*
\p \v 24 Then Annas sent Him,  \add still \add* bound, to Caiaphas the high priest.
\s1 Peter’s Second and Third Denials
\r (\ref Matthew 26:71–75|MAT 26:71-75\ref*; \ref Mark 14:69–72|MRK 14:69-72\ref*; \ref Luke 22:58–62|LUK 22:58-62\ref*)
\p \v 25 Simon Peter was still standing and warming himself. So they asked him, “Aren’t you also \add one\add* of His disciples?”
\p He denied \add it\add* and said, “I am not.”
\p \v 26 One of the high priest’s servants, a relative of \add the man\add* whose ear Peter had cut off, asked, “Didn’t I see you with Him in the garden?”
\p \v 27 Peter denied \add it\add* once more, and immediately a rooster crowed.
\s1 Jesus before Pilate
\r (\ref Matthew 27:11–14|MAT 27:11-14\ref*; \ref Luke 23:1–5|LUK 23:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 28 Then they led Jesus away from Caiaphas into the Praetorium. By now it was early morning, and \add the Jews did\add* not enter the Praetorium, to avoid being defiled \add and unable\add* to eat the Passover.
\p \v 29 So Pilate went out to them and asked, “What accusation are you bringing against this man?”
\p \v 30 “If He were not a criminal,” they replied, “we would not have handed Him over to you.”
\p \v 31 “You take Him and judge Him by your \add own\add* law,” Pilate told them.
\p “We are not permitted to execute anyone,” the Jews replied. \v 32 This was to fulfill the word that Jesus had spoken to indicate the kind of death He was going to die.\f + \fr 18:32 \ft See \ref John 12:32–33|JHN 12:32-33\ref*.\f*
\p \v 33 Pilate went back into the Praetorium, summoned Jesus, and asked Him, “Are You the King of the Jews?”
\p \v 34 \wj “\add Are\add* you saying this on your own,”\wj* Jesus asked, \wj “or \add did\add* others tell you about Me?”\wj*
\p \v 35 “Am I a Jew?” Pilate replied. “Your \add own\add* people and chief priests handed You \add over\add* to me. What have You done?”
\p \v 36 Jesus answered, \wj “My kingdom is not of this world; if \add it\add* were, My servants would fight to prevent My arrest by the Jews. But now My kingdom is not of this realm.”\wj*
\p \v 37 “Then You are a king!” Pilate said.
\p \wj “You say that I am a king,”\wj* Jesus answered. \wj “For this reason I was born and have come into the world, to testify to the truth. Everyone who belongs to the truth listens to My voice.”\wj*
\p \v 38 “What is truth?” Pilate asked.
\p And having said this, he went out again to the Jews and told them, “I find no basis for a charge against Him. \v 39 But it is your custom that I release to you one \add prisoner\add* at the Passover. So then, do you want me to release to you the King of the Jews?”
\p \v 40 “Not this \add man\add*,” they shouted, “but Barabbas!” (Now Barabbas was an insurrectionist.)
\c 19
\s1 The Soldiers Mock Jesus
\r (\ref Isaiah 50:4–11|ISA 50:4-11\ref*; \ref Matthew 27:27–31|MAT 27:27-31\ref*; \ref Mark 15:16–20|MRK 15:16-20\ref*; \ref Luke 22:63–65|LUK 22:63-65\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then Pilate took Jesus and had \add Him\add* flogged. \v 2 The soldiers twisted together a crown of thorns, set \add it\add* on His head, and dressed Him in a purple robe. \v 3 And they went up to Him \add again and again\add*, saying, “Hail, King of the Jews!” and slapping Him in the face.
\p \v 4 \add Once\add* again Pilate came out and said to \add the Jews\add*, “Look, I am bringing Him out to you to let you know that I find no basis for a charge against Him.” \v 5 \add When\add* Jesus came out wearing the crown of thorns and the purple robe, \add Pilate\add* said to them, “\add Here is\add* the man!”
\p \v 6 As soon as the chief priests and officers saw Him, they shouted, “Crucify \add Him\add*! Crucify \add Him\add*!”
\p “You take Him and crucify \add Him\add*,” Pilate replied, “for I find no basis for a charge against Him.”
\p \v 7 “We have a law,” answered the Jews, “and according to \add that\add* law He must die, because He declared Himself \add to be the\add* Son of God.”
\p \v 8 When Pilate heard this statement, he was even more afraid, \v 9 and he went back into the Praetorium. “Where are You from?” he asked.
\p But Jesus gave no answer.
\p \v 10 So Pilate said to Him, “Do You refuse to speak to me? Do You not know that I have authority to release You and authority to crucify You?”
\p \v 11 Jesus answered, \wj “You would have no authority over Me if it were not given to you from above. Therefore the \add one who\add* handed Me over to you is \add guilty of\add* greater sin.”\wj*
\p \v 12 From then on, Pilate tried to release Him, but the Jews kept shouting, “If you release this \add man\add*, you are no friend of Caesar. Anyone who declares himself a king is defying Caesar.”
\p \v 13 \add When\add* Pilate heard these words, he brought Jesus out and sat on \add the\add* judgment seat at a place called \add the\add* Stone Pavement, \add which\add* in Hebrew\f + \fr 19:13 \ft Or \fqa in Aramaic\ft ; also in verses 17 and 20\f* \add is\add* Gabbatha. \v 14 It was the day of Preparation for the Passover, about the sixth hour.\f + \fr 19:14 \ft That is, about noon\f* And \add Pilate\add* said to the Jews, “Here is your King!”
\p \v 15 At this, they shouted, “Away with Him! Away \add with Him\add*! Crucify Him!”
\p “Shall I crucify your King?” Pilate asked.
\p “We have no king but Caesar,” replied the chief priests.
\s1 The Crucifixion
\r (\ref Psalm 22:1–31|PSA 22:1-31\ref*; \ref Matthew 27:32–44|MAT 27:32-44\ref*; \ref Mark 15:21–32|MRK 15:21-32\ref*; \ref Luke 23:26–43|LUK 23:26-43\ref*)
\p \v 16 Then \add Pilate\add* handed \add Jesus\add* over to be crucified, \add and the soldiers\add* took \add Him away\add*. \v 17 Carrying \add His\add* own cross, He went out to \add The\add* Place of the Skull, which in Hebrew is called Golgotha.
\p \v 18 \add There\add* they crucified Him, and with Him two others, \add one\add* on each side, \add with\add* Jesus in the middle.
\p \v 19 Pilate also had a notice posted on the cross. It read:
\pc JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS.
\p \v 20 Many of the Jews read this sign, because the place where Jesus was crucified was near the city, and it was written in Hebrew, Latin, \add and\add* Greek. \v 21 So the chief priests of the Jews said to Pilate, “\add Do\add* not write, ‘The King of the Jews,’ but only that He said, ‘I am \add the\add* King of the Jews.’ ”
\p \v 22 Pilate answered, “What I have written, I have written.”
\p \v 23 When the soldiers had crucified Jesus, they divided His garments \add into\add* four parts, \add one\add* for each soldier, \add with\add* the tunic \add remaining\add*. \add It\add* was seamless, woven in one piece from top to bottom. \v 24 So they said to one another, “Let us not tear it. Instead, let us cast lots to see who \add will get\add* it.” This was to fulfill the Scripture:
\b 
\q1 “They divided My garments among them,
\q2 and cast lots for My clothing.”\f + \fr 19:24 \ref Psalm 22:18|PSA 22:18\ref*\f*
\p So that \add is what\add* the soldiers did.
\p \v 25 Near the cross of Jesus stood His mother and \add her\add* sister, \add as well as\add* Mary the \add wife\add* of Clopas and Mary Magdalene. \v 26 \add When\add* Jesus saw \add His\add* mother and the disciple whom He loved standing nearby, He said to \add His\add* mother, \wj “Woman, here \add is\add* your son.”\wj* \v 27 Then He said to the disciple, \wj “Here \add is\add* your mother.”\wj* So from that hour, \add this\add* disciple took her into \add his home\add*.
\s1 The Death of Jesus
\r (\ref Psalm 22:1–31|PSA 22:1-31\ref*; \ref Matthew 27:45–56|MAT 27:45-56\ref*; \ref Mark 15:33–41|MRK 15:33-41\ref*; \ref Luke 23:44–49|LUK 23:44-49\ref*)
\p \v 28 After this, knowing that everything had now been accomplished, \add and\add* to fulfill the Scripture, Jesus said, \wj “I am thirsty.”\wj*\f + \fr 19:28 \ft See \ref Psalm 22:15|PSA 22:15\ref*.\f* \v 29 A jar of sour wine\f + \fr 19:29 \ft Or \fqa A jar of wine vinegar\ft ; similarly in verse 30\f* was sitting \add there\add*. So they soaked a sponge \add in the\add* wine, put \add it\add* on a stalk of hyssop, \add and\add* lifted it to \add His\add* mouth.\f + \fr 19:29 \ft See \ref Psalm 69:21|PSA 69:21\ref*.\f* \v 30 When Jesus had received the sour wine, He said, \wj “It is finished.”\wj* And bowing \add His\add* head, He yielded up \add His\add* spirit.
\s1 Jesus’ Side Is Pierced
\r (\ref Zechariah 12:10–14|ZEC 12:10-14\ref*)
\p \v 31 It was \add the\add* day of Preparation, \add and the next\add* day was a High Sabbath. In order that the bodies would not remain on the cross during the Sabbath, the Jews asked Pilate to have the legs broken and \add the bodies\add* removed. \v 32 So the soldiers came and broke the legs of the first \add man\add* who had been crucified with \add Jesus\add*, and \add those\add* of the other.
\p \v 33 But when they came to Jesus \add and\add* saw that He was already dead, they did not break His legs. \v 34 Instead, one of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear, and immediately blood and water flowed out. \v 35 The \add one who\add* saw \add it\add* has testified to this, and his testimony is true. He knows that he is telling \add the\add* truth, so that you also may believe.
\p \v 36 Now these things happened so that the Scripture would be fulfilled: “Not one of His bones will be broken.”\f + \fr 19:36 \ref Psalm 34:20|PSA 34:20\ref*; see also Exodus 12:46 and Numbers 9:12.\f* \v 37 And, as another Scripture says: “They will look on the \add One\add* they have pierced.”\f + \fr 19:37 \ref Zechariah 12:10|ZEC 12:10\ref*\f*
\s1 The Burial of Jesus
\r (\ref Isaiah 53:9–12|ISA 53:9-12\ref*; \ref Matthew 27:57–61|MAT 27:57-61\ref*; \ref Mark 15:42–47|MRK 15:42-47\ref*; \ref Luke 23:50–56|LUK 23:50-56\ref*)
\p \v 38 Afterward, Joseph of Arimathea, who was a disciple of Jesus (but secretly for fear of the Jews), asked Pilate to let him remove the body of Jesus. Pilate gave him permission, so he came and removed His body. \v 39 Nicodemus, who had previously come to \add Jesus\add* at night, also brought a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about seventy-five pounds.\f + \fr 19:39 \ft Greek \fqa about a hundred litras\ft ; that is, approximately 34 kilograms\f* \v 40 So they took the body of Jesus and wrapped it in linen cloths with the spices, according to \add the\add* Jewish burial custom.
\p \v 41 Now there was a garden in the place where \add Jesus\add* was crucified, and in the garden a new tomb in which no one had yet been laid. \v 42 And because it was the Jewish day of Preparation \add and\add* the tomb was nearby, they placed Jesus there.
\c 20
\s1 The Resurrection
\r (\ref Matthew 28:1–10|MAT 28:1-10\ref*; \ref Mark 16:1–8|MRK 16:1-8\ref*; \ref Luke 24:1–12|LUK 24:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 1 Early \add on\add* the first \add day\add* of the week,\f + \fr 20:1 \ft Literally \fqa Now on the first of the Sabbaths, early,\f* while it was still dark, Mary Magdalene went to the tomb and saw that the stone had been removed from the \add entrance\add*. \v 2 So she came running to Simon Peter and the other disciple, the one whom Jesus loved. “They have taken the Lord out of the tomb,” she said, “and we do not know where they have put Him!”
\p \v 3 Then Peter and the other disciple set out for the tomb. \v 4 The two were running together, \add but\add* the other disciple outran Peter and reached the tomb first. \v 5 He bent down \add and\add* looked in at the linen cloths lying \add there\add*, but he did not go in.
\p \v 6 Simon Peter arrived just after him. He entered the tomb and saw the linen cloths lying \add there\add*. \v 7 The cloth\f + \fr 20:7 \ft Greek \fqa soudarion\f* that had been around \add Jesus’\add* head was rolled up, lying separate from the linen cloths. \v 8 Then the other disciple, who had reached the tomb first, also went in. And he saw and believed. \v 9 For they still did not understand from the Scripture that \add Jesus\add* had to rise from \add the\add* dead.
\s1 Jesus Appears to Mary Magdalene
\r (\ref Mark 16:9–11|MRK 16:9-11\ref*)
\p \v 10 Then the disciples returned to their \add homes\add*. \v 11 But Mary stood outside the tomb weeping. \add And\add* as she wept, she bent down \add to look\add* into the tomb, \v 12 and she saw two angels in white sitting where the body of Jesus had lain, one at the head and \add the other\add* at the feet.
\p \v 13 “Woman, why are you weeping?” they asked.
\p “Because they have taken my Lord \add away\add*,” she said, “and I do not know where they have put Him.”
\p \v 14 When she had said this, she turned around and saw Jesus standing \add there\add*. \add But\add* she did not recognize that it was Jesus.
\p \v 15 \wj “Woman, why are you weeping?”\wj* Jesus asked. \wj “Whom are you seeking?”\wj*
\p Thinking He was the gardener, she said, “Sir, if you have carried Him off, tell me where you have put Him, and I will get Him.”
\p \v 16 Jesus said to her, \wj “Mary.”\wj*
\p She turned \add and\add* said to Him in Hebrew,\f + \fr 20:16 \ft Or \fqa in Aramaic\f* “Rabboni!” (which means “Teacher”).
\p \v 17 \wj “\add Do\add* not cling to Me,”\wj* Jesus said, \wj “for I have not yet ascended to the Father. But go and tell My brothers, ‘I am ascending to My Father and your Father, to My God and your God.’”\wj*
\p \v 18 Mary Magdalene went and announced to the disciples, “I have seen the Lord!” And \add she told them what\add* He had said to her.
\s1 Jesus Appears to the Disciples
\r (\ref Luke 24:36–49|LUK 24:36-49\ref*; \ref 1 John 1:1–4|1JN 1:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 19 It was the first day of \add the\add* week, \add and that\add* very evening, while the disciples were \add together\add* with the doors locked for fear of the Jews, Jesus came and stood among them. \wj “Peace \add be\add* with you!”\wj* He said to them. \v 20 \add After\add* He had said this, He showed them His hands and His side.
\p The disciples rejoiced when they saw the Lord.
\p \v 21 Again Jesus said to them, \wj “Peace \add be\add* with you. As the Father has sent Me, so also I am sending you.”\wj* \v 22 \add When\add* He had said this, He breathed on \add them\add* and said, \wj “Receive \add the\add* Holy Spirit.\wj* \v 23 \wj If you forgive anyone his sins, they are forgiven; if you withhold \add forgiveness\add* from anyone, it is withheld.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Appears to Thomas
\p \v 24 Now Thomas called Didymus,\f + \fr 20:24 \fqa Didymus \ft means \fqa the twin\ft .\f* one of the Twelve, was not with \add the disciples\add* when Jesus came. \v 25 So the other disciples told him, “We have seen the Lord!”
\p But he replied, “Unless I see the nail marks in His hands, and put my finger \add where\add* the nails \add have been\add*, and put my hand into His side, I will never believe.”
\p \v 26 Eight days later, His disciples were once again inside \add with\add* the doors locked, and Thomas \add was\add* with them. Jesus came and stood among them and said, \wj “Peace \add be\add* with you.”\wj*
\p \v 27 Then \add Jesus\add* said to Thomas, \wj “Put your finger here and look at My hands. Reach out your hand and put \add it\add* into My side. Stop doubting and believe.”\wj*
\p \v 28 Thomas replied, “My Lord and my God!”
\p \v 29 Jesus said to him, \wj “Because you have seen Me, you have believed; blessed \add are\add* those who have not seen and yet have believed.”\wj*
\s1 The Purpose of John’s Book
\p \v 30 Jesus performed many other signs in the presence of His disciples, which are not written in this book. \v 31 But these are written so that you may believe\f + \fr 20:31 \ft Or \fqa may continue to believe\f* that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that by believing you may have life in His name.
\c 21
\s1 Jesus Appears by the Sea of Tiberias
\p \v 1 Later, by the Sea of Tiberias,\f + \fr 21:1 \ft That is, the Sea of Galilee\f* Jesus again revealed Himself to the disciples. He made \add Himself\add* known in this way: \v 2 Simon Peter, Thomas called Didymus,\f + \fr 21:2 \fqa Didymus \ft means \fqa the twin\ft .\f* Nathanael from Cana in Galilee, the \add sons\add* of Zebedee, and two other disciples were together. \v 3 Simon Peter told them, “I am going fishing.”
\p “We will go with you,” they said. \add So\add* they went out and got into the boat, \add but\add* caught nothing that night.
\p \v 4 Early in the morning, Jesus stood on the shore, but the disciples \add did\add* not recognize that it was Jesus. \v 5 So \add He\add* called out to them, \wj “Children, do you have any fish?”\wj*
\p “No,” they answered.
\p \v 6 He told them, \wj “Cast the net on the right side of the boat, and you will find \add some\add*.”\wj* So they cast \add it there\add*, and they were unable to haul it in because of the great number of fish.
\p \v 7 Then \add the\add* disciple whom Jesus loved said to Peter, “It is the Lord!” As soon as Simon Peter heard that it was the Lord, he put on \add his\add* outer garment (for he had removed it . . .) and jumped into the sea. \v 8 The other disciples came \add ashore\add* in the boat. They dragged in the net full of fish, for they were not far from land, only about a hundred yards.\f + \fr 21:8 \ft Greek \fqa about two hundred cubits away\ft ; that is, approximately 300 feet or 91 meters\f*
\p \v 9 When they landed, they saw a charcoal fire there \add with\add* fish on it, and \add some\add* bread.
\p \v 10 Jesus told them, \wj “Bring \add some\add* of the fish you have just caught.”\wj* \v 11 So Simon Peter went aboard and dragged the net ashore. \add It was\add* full of large fish, 153, but even with so many, the net was not torn.
\p \v 12 \wj “Come, have breakfast,”\wj* Jesus said to them. None of the disciples dared to ask Him, “Who are You?” They knew it was the Lord. \v 13 Jesus came and took the bread and gave \add it\add* to them, and \add He did\add* the same with the fish.
\p \v 14 This \add was\add* now the third time \add that\add* Jesus appeared to the disciples after He was raised from \add the\add* dead.
\s1 Jesus and Peter
\p \v 15 When they had finished eating, Jesus asked Simon Peter, \wj “Simon \add son\add* of John, do you love Me more than these?”\wj*
\p “Yes, Lord,” he answered, “You know I love You.”
\p \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “Feed My lambs.”\wj*
\p \v 16 \add Jesus\add* asked a second time, \wj “Simon \add son\add* of John, do you love Me?”\wj*
\p “Yes, Lord,” he answered, “You know I love You.”
\p \add Jesus\add* told him, \wj “Shepherd My sheep.”\wj*
\p \v 17 \add Jesus\add* asked a third time, \wj “Simon \add son\add* of John, do you love Me?”\wj*
\p Peter was deeply hurt that \add Jesus\add* had asked him a third time, \wj “Do you love Me?”\wj*
\p “Lord, You know all things,” he replied. “You know I love You.”
\p Jesus said to him, \wj “Feed My sheep.\wj*
\p \v 18 \wj Truly, truly, I tell you, when you were young, you dressed yourself and walked where you wanted; but when you are old, you will stretch out your hands, and someone else\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Or \fqa others\f* will dress you and lead \add you\add* where you do not want to go.”\wj* \v 19 \add Jesus\add* said this to indicate \add the kind of\add* death by which \add Peter\add* would glorify God.
\p And \add after\add* He had said this, He told him, \wj “Follow Me.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus and the Beloved Disciple
\p \v 20 Peter turned \add and\add* saw the disciple whom Jesus loved following \add them\add*. \add He was the one\add* who had leaned back \add against Jesus\add*\f + \fr 21:20 \ft Greek \fqa reclined on His bosom\f* at the supper to ask, “Lord, who is going to betray You?” \v 21 \add When Peter\add* saw him, \add he\add* asked, “Lord, what about \add him\add*?”
\p \v 22 Jesus answered, \wj “If I want him to remain until I return, what \add is that\add* to you? You follow Me!”\wj* \v 23 Because of this, the rumor spread among the brothers that this disciple \add would\add* not die. However, Jesus did not say that he would not die, but only, \wj “If I want him to remain until I return, what \add is that\add* to you?”\wj*
\p \v 24 This is the disciple who testifies to these things and who has written \add them down\add*. And we know that his testimony is true.
\p \v 25 There are many more things that Jesus did. If all of them were written down, I suppose that not even the world itself would have space for the books that would be written.
